\begin{proof}
\paragraph{2. Exhaustion from the defining raw profiles.}

Use the ordered matrices, section cocycles, component coordinates
and heights of Definition~\ref{mapsIII:def:elliptic-marked-data}. For a multiplicative site,
choose the primitive vector \(u\) with first nonzero entry positive and
the Bezout complement \(v\) so that
\begin{equation}
 A-I=\nu u u^tJ,\quad g=\kappa u,\quad
 J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad\det(u,v)=1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22E}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_5}
\end{equation}
The integer \(\nu\) is the content of \(A-I\). If
\(x u_1+y u_2=1\), take \(v=(-y,x)^t\).
For height \(1/d\), a natural selected zero requires \(ab=d/\nu\)
with \(a,b>0\), \(\gcd(a,b)=1\), \(\gcd(b\kappa,\nu)=1\), and the literal
component equality
\begin{equation}
 e_{a\kappa}-e_0-e_{(a-b)\kappa}+e_{-b\kappa}
                =-2e_0+e_1+e_{-1}\quad\text{in }\mathbb Z[\mathbb Z/\nu].

\hypertarget{tag-Q22F}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_6}
\end{equation}
Repeated residues are added. This retains the \(\nu=1,2\) cases.
At each other reducible site one of \(aG,bG\) is narrow. In the marked
component group this means every listed coordinate vanishes modulo its
listed modulus. These are the necessary and sufficient natural-row
conditions of \ref{maps:src:D.2.22}. Its actual map
multiplies the original line vector by the chord and vertical-divisor
quotient divided by the rational base function; its inverse multiplies
by the reciprocal. The small contracting constant and the nonzero
period-unit functions stay in that construction.

The positive-valuation Tate labelling sends the input component label
\(j\) to \(-j\). This bijection is its own inverse. Applying it to every
term of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_6} gives the physical component equation; its right side is
fixed by the reflection. Thus this enumeration retains the physical sign of \(G\)
through the displayed reflection.

An exact enumeration proceeds through every divisor pair and evaluates
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_6} as an integer vector, then checks every unselected component. It
recovers the following 21 natural-row counts, summing to 172:
\[
 \begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrrrrr}
 &\mathrm{OS43}&\mathrm{OS43}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS45}&\mathrm{OS45}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS46}&\mathrm{OS46}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS47}&\mathrm{OS47}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS47}/\mathrm{III}&\mathrm{OS49}&\mathrm{OS49}/\mathrm{II}\\
 \#&6&2&8&4&6&2&14&6&8&10&2\\ \hline
 &\mathrm{OS49}/\mathrm{III}&\mathrm{OS50}&\mathrm{OS50}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS50}/\mathrm{IV}&\mathrm{OS55}&\mathrm{OS55}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS56}&\mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{III}&\mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{IV}\\
 \#&4&10&2&4&14&6&26&10&14&14
 \end{array}
\]
At a finite additive site of type \(II,III,IV,III^*,IV^*\), respectively,
the finite order and vertical coinvariant index are
\begin{equation}
 (d_i,q_i)=(6,1),(4,2),(3,3),(4,2),(3,3).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22G}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_7}
\end{equation}
The exact finite lattice condition is
\(b(A_i-I)^{-1}g_i\in\mathbb Z^2\). A minimal original additive cap
occurs at \(II,III,IV\) when \(aG\) is narrow and \(bG\) has one component
orbit; the latter is \(\gcd(b,q_i)=1\) in these cyclic cases.
The cap and rank maps of \ref{maps:src:D.2.26} prove these criteria. Finite orders
at different sites are pairwise coprime for a simply connected output,
and
\begin{equation}
                    c=b/\prod_{i\in F}q_i.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22H}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_8}
\end{equation}
Expanding these choices over the sixteen nonstar profiles and
retaining $c\in\{6,15,30\}$ gives exactly the fourteen patterns
in \eqref{maps:eq:D.MT1}.

Only three defining profiles occur. Their ordered fibre lists and
primitive multiplicative markings are as follows:
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|l|l}
 \text{profile}&\text{fibres}&(\nu,\kappa)\text{ at multiplicative sites}\\\hline
 \mathrm{OS56}&I_5,I_3,I_2,I_1,I_1&(5,-4),(3,-2),(2,-1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\
 \mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{II}&I_2,II,I_5,I_3&(2,-1),\ --,\ (5,1),(3,1)\\
 \mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{III}&I_5,I_3,III,I_1&(5,-4),(3,-1),\ --,\ (1,-1).
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q22I}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_9}
\end{equation}
Each of these profiles has generator height $1/30$ and at most
one additive site. Its matrices and cocycles are the ordered entries
of Definition~\ref{mapsIII:def:elliptic-marked-data}.

\paragraph{3. Actual period maps and integral exterior coordinates.}

Keep
\begin{equation}
 \Lambda=\mathbb Z\langle e_1,e_2,\delta,\gamma\rangle,\qquad
 \omega=a e_1^*\wedge e_2^*-b\delta^*\wedge\gamma^*,\quad
 \chi=\gamma^*.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22J}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_10}
\end{equation}
Positive \(\gamma\) is the forward contracting arrow covering \(-bG\).
The scalar circle is \(x\mapsto x+\theta\delta\), \(\theta\in\mathbb R/\mathbb Z\).
At a multiplicative site put \(z_i=(\kappa_i/\nu_i)v_i\); at an additive
site put \(z_i=(A_i-I)^{-1}g_i\). Then
\begin{equation}
 \beta_i=-a g_i^tJA_i,\quad
 c_i=\beta_i b z_i+w_i,\quad
 T_i=\begin{pmatrix}A_i&0&bg_i\\\beta_i&1&c_i\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad
 w_i=\begin{cases}1/\nu_i&i\text{ selected},\\0&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}

\hypertarget{tag-Q22K}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_11}
\end{equation}
The inverse has blocks
\begin{equation}
 T_i^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
 A_i^{-1}&0&-bA_i^{-1}g_i\\
 -\beta_iA_i^{-1}&1&b\beta_iA_i^{-1}g_i-c_i\\0&0&1
 \end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22L}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_12}
\end{equation}
Substitution gives integral matrices and both inverse products,
$T_i^*\omega=\omega$, $T_i\delta=\delta$, $\chi T_i=\chi$,
and the ordered product $\prod T_i=I$.

These matrices act on the same actual germs. On a cut base, the period
map is
\begin{equation}
 x\bmod\Lambda\longmapsto\Pi(q)x\bmod\Pi(q)\Lambda.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22M}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_13}
\end{equation}
Its inverse is the elliptic and line logarithm modulo that same lattice.
Here \(\Pi(q)\) contains the original nonzero holomorphic units. An
integral basis \(H\) replaces \(\Pi\) by \(\Pi H\) and coordinates by
\(H^{-1}x\). The inverse uses \(H\) and the inverse logarithm. These maps
retain the base coordinate, zero section, contraction and collars.

Use wedge order \(12,13,14,23,24,34\). Choose \(ax+by=1\) and put
\(s_0=x e_{12}-y e_{34}\). Set
\[
 B_2=[\wedge^2T_1-I\ \cdots\ \wedge^2T_N-I].
\]
Tracked integer row and column reduction gives a section $R_2$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 B_2R_2=I-s_0\omega,\qquad \omega B_2=0,\qquad\omega(s_0)=1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22N}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_14}
\end{equation}
Its columns give the monodromy-cylinder preimages. The inverse quotient maps
are
\begin{equation}
 \wedge^2\Lambda/\operatorname{im}B_2
 \ \underset{n\mapsto[ns_0]}{\overset{[v]\mapsto\omega(v)}{\rightleftarrows}}
 \mathbb Z\langle s\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22O}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_15}
\end{equation}
The difference \(v-\omega(v)s_0\) bounds the actual monodromy cylinders
with coefficients \(R_2v\). There are five unit Smith entries, so no
integral index is omitted.

Include the removed ordinary disc as the last site, with \(T=I\).
For \(D=[T_1-I\ \cdots\ T_N-I]\), the first \(N\) boundary maps are
block inclusions. The last is
\begin{equation}
 j_{N+1}(u)=-(T_2\cdots T_Nu,\ldots,T_Nu,u).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22P}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_16}
\end{equation}
It is obtained by tracing the actual polygon boundary, with its boundary
orientation. Its \(D\)-image is \(I-T_1\cdots T_N=0\).
At every cap take its actual fixed integral basis beginning with \(\delta\).
For $B=[j_iF_i]$, the tracked integer reductions give $V_0,Q_0,R_G$ with
\begin{equation}
 DV_0=0,\quad Q_0V_0=I,\quad BR_G=V_0Q_0,\quad
 \operatorname{im}V_0=\ker D,\quad
 \ker B=\mathbb Z\sum_i\delta_i.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22Q}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_17}
\end{equation}
Tracked elementary integer operations give a saturated kernel \(V_0\)
of $D$; its three nonzero Smith entries are one. The same reduction
of $B$ has unit nonzero Smith entries and nullity one. The displayed kernel vector has coefficient one in every
scalar position and is primitive.

Fixed loops sweep base boundary circles to two-tori. Addition is realized
by the period triangle with vertices \(0,u,u+v\); the opposite triangle
is the inverse chain. Projecting to the graph, using \(R_G\), subtracting
those sweeps and then using \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_14} gives inverse coordinates
\begin{equation}
 H_2(\mathcal E)=\mathbb Zs\oplus
 \left(\bigoplus_i\operatorname{Fix}(T_i)\right)/
                    \mathbb Z\sum_i\delta_i.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22R}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_18}
\end{equation}
A line-frame change on the scalar sweep contributes
\(\delta\wedge\delta=0\). Clutching by \(e\gamma\) changes the polygon
relation by \(-\omega(\delta,e\gamma)=be\).

\paragraph{4. Cap maps, arbitrary finite parameters, and homology.}

At a rank-one multiplicative cap use the basis \(H=(l,h,p_0,w_0)\)
constructed in the primitive rank-one construction. The actual normalized ruled component with
its two sections identified gives
\begin{equation}
 f_1(x)=(x_2,x_3,x_4),\qquad
 f_2(y)=(y_{12},y_{23},y_{24},y_{34}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22S}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_19}
\end{equation}
Inserting zero in the omitted coordinates and applying \(H\) or
\(\wedge^2H\) gives sections. The fibre kernel is generated by
\(e_{13},e_{14}\), with actual monodromy-cylinder preimages \(e_{23},e_{24}\).
Fixed sweeps bound the normal toric chains.

At the cusp use \(H=(SCe_1,Le_1,SCe_2,Le_2)\) of the prescribed-cusp construction. Then
\begin{equation}
 f_1(x)=(x_2,x_4),\qquad
 f_2(y)=(y_{12},y_{14}-y_{23},y_{24},y_{34}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22T}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_20}
\end{equation}
A section of the second map inserts \((u,0,v,0,w,z)\) and applies
\(\wedge^2H\). The primitive kernel is \(e_{13},e_{14}+e_{23}\);
monodromy-cylinder preimages are \(e_{23}\) and \(e_{24}-e_{23}\).
The normalization/conductor sequence gives these cycles and
the normal chains bounding both fixed sweeps. Substitution in the
displayed sections gives the identity and their Wang-image vanishing.

For an original additive cap, \(h=a(A-I)^{-1}g\) is integral. Set
\(\lambda=-h^tJ\). The line-frame map and inverse are
\begin{equation}
 S_P(x,d,g_0)=(x,d+\lambda x,g_0),\qquad
 S_P^{-1}(x,d',g_0)=(x,d'-\lambda x,g_0).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22U}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_21}
\end{equation}
The identity \(\lambda(A-I)+\beta=0\) removes the line shear on
the actual neighbourhood. Zero line multidegree gives a topological
line trivialization there. The positive-roof mapping torus for the
contracting arrow has a unit-roof parametrization: choose a smooth
nonnegative bump \(b_y\) of integral one on a roof of length \(r(y)\),
choose \(0<k<(\max r)^{-1}\), and use the increasing map
\begin{equation}
 f_y(t)=\int_0^t\{k+(1-kr(y))b_y(v)\}\,dv

\hypertarget{tag-Q22V}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_22}
\end{equation}
and its inverse. The ends have matching derivatives. Translation by
the identity-component Neron logarithm times the roof coordinate,
with inverse negative translation and a cutoff at the ends, removes
that part of the return. The finite component translation has one
orbit. Reciprocal line gauges retain its actual node constants.
These inverse maps identify the original cap and its indicated mapping torus.

Put \(z_Q=b(A-I)^{-1}g\) and \(q=1\) for \(II\), \(q=2\) for \(III\).
The original core has \(H_2\) basis \([C],[\delta\gamma]\), with
elliptic fibre \(q[C]\). In coordinates after \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_21}, its map is
\begin{equation}
 f_2(y)=\bigl(q(y_{12}+z_{Q,2}y_{14}-z_{Q,1}y_{24}),y_{34}\bigr).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22W}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_23}
\end{equation}
The first coordinate is the pullback under \(x\mapsto x+z_Qx_\gamma\),
whose inverse subtracts that term. An integral Bezout solution of
\(qA+qz_{Q,2}B-qz_{Q,1}C=1\), followed by \(\wedge^2S_P^{-1}\),
gives a section on \([C]\); \(e_{34}\) gives the other section.
These two sections recover the stated generators. The fixed basis is
\begin{equation}
                  \delta,\quad\ell_0=(-qz_{Q,1},-qz_{Q,2},0,q).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22X}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_24}
\end{equation}
For original III in row 9 it is
\(\ell_0=(-15,45,0,2)\). Its two sweeps bound the origin disc and
the two-fold forward orbit tube closed by the same logarithm and line
frame. The gamma index is retained.

At a finite cap, \(\ell=(-bz_1,-bz_2,0,1)\) is integral and
\begin{equation}
 m=d_i k>0,\qquad v=n\ell+r\delta,\qquad
 \gcd(m,n)=1,\quad r\in\mathbb Z.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22Y}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_25}
\end{equation}
For finite II, \(\ell=\gamma,\beta_0=\delta,q=1\). For finite III in
row 8,
\begin{equation}
 \ell=(-15,45,0,1),\quad\beta_0=(0,-1,0,0),\quad q=2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22Z}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_26}
\end{equation}
Integer reduction of $[T-I\mid\beta_0\mid\ell]$ gives a right
inverse and an integral $y$ with
\begin{equation}
 q\beta_0+(T-I)y=\delta,\quad
 N_{d_i}\beta_0=(d_i/q)\delta,\quad
 N_{d_i}\ell=d_i\ell,\quad-\omega(\beta_0,\ell)=b/q.

\hypertarget{tag-Q230}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_27}
\end{equation}
Its last two inverse rows give the primitive coinvariant coordinates.
This distinguishes the gamma-one finite lift \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_26} from \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_24}.

The actual affine representative is \(R(x)=Tx+v/m\). Another lift
\(Tx+t\) with \(N_mt=v\) is conjugate to this one by \(x\mapsto x+h\),
where \((I-T)h=t-v/m\) on the complement of
\(\pi=N_m/m\); \(I-T\) is invertible on that complement. Translation
by \(-h\) is the inverse and commutes with scalar translation.
For the actual holomorphic units on the whole good root disc, let
\(\mathcal Rf(u)=A(\sigma^{-1}u)f(\sigma^{-1}u)\), where
\(\sigma(u)=\zeta u\) and \(\mathcal R^m=I\). If the two affine terms
differ by \(d\) with \(\mathcal Nd=0\), use
\begin{equation}
 h=-\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}j\mathcal R^jd,\quad
 (I-\mathcal R)h=d.

\hypertarget{tag-Q231}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_28}
\end{equation}
The inverse is \(-h\), so the comparison is over the disc and its collar,
with the units present. A peripheral period change uses the actual norm
\(v\mapsto v+N_m\lambda\), with fixed-sweep shear
\(t(u)\mapsto t(u)+\omega(u,\lambda)s\). The inverse uses \(-\lambda\).
The expression \(m\lambda\) is used only for a fixed \(\lambda\).

The boundary map and its inverse in the chosen affine representative are
\begin{equation}
 [y,\tau]\longmapsto[y+(\tau/m)v,e^{2\pi i\tau/m}],\qquad
 [x,z]\longmapsto[x-(\tau/m)v,\tau].

\hypertarget{tag-Q232}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_29}
\end{equation}
A different root argument changes both sides by the mapping-torus
relation. The positive normal meridian is \(m\mu-v\).
Actual sectors of its meridional disc and the period parallelograms give,
in \((s,D=t(\delta),L=t(\ell))\), the full integral Thom columns
\begin{equation}
 C_1=(bn/q,m/q,0),\quad C_2=(-br,0,m),\quad C_3=(0,r,n).

\hypertarget{tag-Q233}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_30}
\end{equation}
The opposite sectors give the inverse boundary chains.

To check their integral scope, the finite central surface has
\begin{equation}
 H_1(F)=\langle\beta_0,\ell,c_0\mid
                  mc_0=n\ell+rq\beta_0\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q234}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_31}
\end{equation}
For \(A_0m+B_0n=1\), the basis
\(\beta_0,z=A_0\ell+B_0c_0\) has inverse
\(\ell=mz-B_0rq\beta_0\), \(c_0=nz+A_0rq\beta_0\).
Thus \(H_1(F)\) is free. The finite torus cover and transfer give rational
Betti numbers \(1,2,2,2,1\); integral duality then makes \(H_2,H_3\)
free as well. The flat normal line has zero Chern class in free
\(H^2(F;\mathbb Z)\). Transfer of \(\lambda\) cuts into sectors with
fibre area \(-\lambda\wedge v\) and sweep \(N_m\lambda\); the torus for
\(c_0\) has fibre area zero and sweep \(v\). Rejoining those sectors
is the inverse chain map. Thom exactness on the basis above gives
exactly \(C_1,A_0C_2+B_0C_3\), with no suppressed index.

There is at most one finite cap. Its full fundamental-group presentation,
after the angular relations of the common global Borel comparison, is
\begin{equation}
 \langle\gamma,c_0\mid[\gamma,c_0]=1,\ c_0^m=\gamma^n,\ c_0=\gamma^e\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q235}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_32}
\end{equation}
Hence simple connectivity is precisely \(me-n=\varepsilon=\pm1\).
In the rows without a finite cap it is \(e=\pm1\).
Choose \(A_0=\varepsilon e,B_0=-\varepsilon\). With
\(K_0=A_0C_2+B_0C_3\), the exact change of column generators is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 K_0&=(-A_0br,B_0r,1),\\
 C_2-mK_0&=-B_0qrC_1,\qquad
 C_3-nK_0=A_0qrC_1.
 \end{split}

\hypertarget{tag-Q236}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_33}
\end{equation}
The ordinary polygon relation is \(D=-be\,s\). It gives \(L=0\) and
\begin{equation}
                   (b/q)(n-me)s=-\varepsilon c\,s=0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q237}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_34}
\end{equation}
Substituting $A_0=\varepsilon e$, $B_0=-\varepsilon$ and
$me-n=\varepsilon$ proves \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_33}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34} for both signs and all
integers $m,e,r$ in the stated domain. For no finite cap the remaining relation is
\(be\,s=0\), and \(c=b\). All cap maps are surjective and \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_17} is
integral, so Mayer--Vietoris gives \(H_2(X)=\mathbb Z/c\langle s\rangle\).
The two cusp vertices give Euler number two, and the other torus strata
and caps have Euler number zero. Duality and universal coefficients give
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_1}. Since \(H^2(X;\mathbb Z)=0\), the complex first Chern class is
zero, so \(w_2=0\); the spin structure is unique because \(H^1(X;\mathbb F_2)=0\).

\paragraph{5. Primitive rank-one periods and all scalar isotropy.}

At an unselected multiplicative site put
\begin{equation}
 d_0=\gcd(\nu,a\kappa),\quad n_0=\nu/d_0,\quad
 A_0=a\kappa/d_0,\quad B_0=b\kappa/n_0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q238}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_35}
\end{equation}
Primitivity makes \(B_0\) integral and \(\gcd(d_0,B_0)=1\).
Choose \(n_0\theta_N+A_0\sigma=1\), \(d_0x+B_0y=1\), and set
\begin{equation}
 C_S=\begin{pmatrix}n_0&\sigma\\-A_0&\theta_N\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C_L=\begin{pmatrix}x&-B_0\\y&d_0\end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q239}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_36}
\end{equation}
Their literal inverses are
\begin{equation}
 C_S^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}\theta_N&-\sigma\\A_0&n_0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C_L^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}d_0&B_0\\-y&x\end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23A}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_37}
\end{equation}
With \(S=(u,\delta),L=(v,\gamma)\), write
\((l,p_0)=SC_S\), \((h,w_0)=LC_L\). The ordered period basis
\(H=(l,h,p_0,w_0)\) has determinant one and return \(h\mapsto h+l\).
The other columns are fixed. Its pairing is
\begin{equation}
 K_N=(SC_S)^t\omega(LC_L)
       =\begin{pmatrix}N&0\\M&R\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 N=\frac{a\nu}{d_0^2},\quad R=-\frac{bd_0^2}{\nu},\qquad
 \delta=-\sigma l+n_0p_0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23B}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_38}
\end{equation}
Their determinants are one; the displayed products with their inverse matrices are the identity.

The actual chart identification is \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_13} in this basis, followed by
exponentiation of \(l\). Consecutive charts and their inverses are
\begin{equation}
 X=z/q^j,\quad Y=q^{j+1}/z,\quad XY=q;\qquad z=Xq^j,\ q=XY.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23C}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_39}
\end{equation}
The primitive long period identifies successive charts with the original
unit multipliers transformed by \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_36}. The inverse uses \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_37} and \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_39}.
The core is one ruled normalization with zero and infinity sections
identified along the elliptic curve of periods \(p_0,w_0\).

On that elliptic curve, the scalar circle translates the first factor
by \(n_0\theta p_0\). Thus a subgroup \(C_p\) fixes it exactly when
\(p\mid n_0\). Otherwise it acts there by a nonzero translation.
At a fixed elliptic curve, away from the double locus, its nonzero
normal coordinate has a unit character, so there is no extra fixed
point on the ruled normalization. Substitution of the ordered
component data gives, for each $p\in\{2,3,5\}$ dividing $c$, exactly one such site,
of type \(I_p\), with
\begin{equation}
 d_0=1,\quad n_0=p,\quad N=ap,\quad R=-b/p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23D}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_40}
\end{equation}
Call its elliptic curve \(E_p\). In the actual subgroup generator its
normal characters are \((-\sigma,\sigma)\). For odd \(p\), the
positive-valuation Tate residue is \(r=-\kappa_i\) and
\begin{equation}
             w=(ar)^{-1}=-\sigma\pmod p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23E}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_41}
\end{equation}
This uses \(\sigma a\kappa_i=1\pmod p\).

The cases \(d_0>1\) are not suppressed: for example the unselected
\(I_5\) at \(a=5,b=6\) has \(d_0=5,n_0=1\). Its scalar translation
has no nontrivial finite stabilizer. The displayed basis and its inverse identify that primitive
conductor and its nontrivial scalar translation.

\paragraph{6. The prescribed cusp and the remaining isotropy.}

At the selected site set
\begin{equation}
 \rho=\frac{1-ab\kappa^2}{\nu},\qquad
 C=\begin{pmatrix}\nu&b\kappa\\-a\kappa&\rho\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}\rho&-b\kappa\\a\kappa&\nu\end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23F}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_42}
\end{equation}
Equation \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_6} makes \(C\) integral with determinant one.
The actual return satisfies \((T-I)L=SC\), \((T-I)S=0\).
The prescribed Jordan fan therefore has short basis \(SC\).
The physical scalar direction in that fan is
\begin{equation}
                  d_\delta=C^{-1}(0,1)^t=(-b\kappa,\nu)^t.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23G}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_43}
\end{equation}
Let \(k=b\kappa\). For each prime \(p\mid c\), \(p\mid k\) and
\(\nu\) is a unit modulo \(p\). Thus \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_43} is vertical and nonzero
modulo \(p\). Among the three edge directions of the prescribed unit
triangulation, only the vertical one is parallel to it modulo \(p\).
There is one such edge orbit. Its compact curve \(C_f\) is described
by the adjacent actual charts
\begin{equation}
 (x,y,w)=(q/(X_1X_2),X_2,X_1),\qquad
 (x',y',w')=(q/X_2,X_1X_2,X_1^{-1}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23H}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_44}
\end{equation}
Their inverses are
\((X_1,X_2,q)=(w,y,xyw)\) and
\((1/w',y'w',x'y'w')\). Their overlap and inverse are
\begin{equation}
 (x',y',w')=(wx,wy,w^{-1}),\qquad
 (x,y,w)=(w'x',w'y',(w')^{-1}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23I}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_45}
\end{equation}
Consequently \(C_f\simeq\mathbb P^1\) is embedded and its normal
bundle is \(\mathcal O(-1)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)\).
A translation stabilizing a bounded edge fixes its endpoints and is
zero, so no additional quotient of this curve occurs.

The full-circle endpoint weights are
\begin{equation}
           (k-\nu,\nu,-k),\qquad(-\nu,\nu-k,k).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23J}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_46}
\end{equation}
For \(k=0\) the circle fixes \(C_f\); otherwise it rotates \(C_f\)
with tangent weight \(-k\). Its generic stabilizer then has order
\(|k|\). In every case \(C_p\) fixes precisely this curve within the
cusp, with opposite nonzero normal characters. The two triangle
orbits give the two endpoints already on \(C_f\).

The scalar circle is free on every original additive cap. In an adapted
norm the forward arrow strictly contracts. A unit-modulus scalar
cannot equal a nonzero iterate on a nonzero line vector; the inverse
iterate strictly expands. The zero iterate fixes only the identity
scalar. The same-germ roof and logarithm maps of the cap-map construction retain
this conclusion.

At a finite cap a scalar fixed point would satisfy, on its good cover,
\(x+\theta\delta=R^j x\) modulo the lattice. Applying the integral
covector \(\chi\) gives \(jn/m\in\mathbb Z\); coprimality forces
\(m\mid j\). The remaining scalar translation is primitive and is
fixed only for integral \(\theta\). Away from the central fibre the
base rotation already gives \(m\mid j\). This proves freeness for
every allowed \(m,n,r\), including \(3\mid m\), and every affine
lift through \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_28}. The ordinary cap is free by the same primitive
scalar translation.

Therefore, on each relevant row,
\begin{equation}
                     X^{C_p}=C_f\amalg E_p\qquad(p\mid c).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23K}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_47}
\end{equation}
Each prime subgroup has free complement. Every nontrivial subgroup
of \(C_9\) contains \(C_3\), so \(C_9\) is free off
\(C_f\amalg E_3\). Its normal sphere bundles there are free, since
their \(C_3\) normal characters are nonzero. These statements do not
require a semifree full-circle action.

\paragraph{7. One actual line, its fan support, and its inverse gauges.}

Let \(Y\) be the partial completion at all multiplicative sites,
leaving the ordinary and additive discs open. It contains all fixed
curves in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_47}.

For every row there is a doubled line \(\mathcal D\) on the regular
period cover with factors
\begin{equation}
 J_\lambda(x,\zeta_R)=(x+\lambda,e^{2\pi i\omega(\lambda,x)}\zeta_R).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23L}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_48}
\end{equation}
The inverse is \(J_{-\lambda}\); the cocycle quotient is
\(e^{2\pi i\omega(\lambda,\mu)}=1\).
The connection \(d-2\pi i\omega(x,dx)\) gives \(c_1=2\omega\).
The actual monodromy lift is \((x,\zeta_R)\mapsto(T_i x,\zeta_R)\), with
inverse \(T_i^{-1}\). For \(p=3,5\) dividing \(c\), the lift of
\(\delta/p\) is
\begin{equation}
 (x,\zeta_R)\longmapsto(x+\delta/p,e^{2\pi i\omega(\delta/p,x)}\zeta_R).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23M}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_49}
\end{equation}
Its commutator with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_48} is
\(e^{-4\pi i b\lambda_\gamma/p}=1\), and its \(p\)-th power is
\(J_\delta\). It commutes with every monodromy lift.

For the nonzero short period $\omega_s$, use
$z_E=z_{\rm add}/\omega_s$, with inverse $z_{\rm add}=\omega_s z_E$.
Put $\tau_E=\omega_l/\omega_s$, $\zeta_G=G_{\rm lift}/\omega_s$,
and $z=e^{2\pi i z_E}$; the inverse logarithm is modulo the short
period. Let $\rho_L$ be the forward line-period logarithm.
The theta ratio
$\vartheta(z_E-a\zeta_G)/\vartheta(z_E)$, with reciprocal inverse,
identifies \(\mathcal O(aG-O)\) with its Poincare factors. Shifting
the theta sum gives short factor one and long factor
\(e^{2\pi i a\zeta_G}\). The forward lift of \(-bG\) has constant
multiplier in this trivialization, because its ratio with the
constant-factor lift is a holomorphic function on the compact elliptic
curve. The constant retains its actual base-dependent unit.

In physical bases \(S=(u,\delta),L=(v,\gamma)\), write
\begin{equation}
 D=\operatorname{diag}(a,-b),\quad
 \Pi_{\rm ph}=\begin{pmatrix}\tau_E&-b\zeta_G\\a\zeta_G&\rho_L\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad D\Pi_{\rm ph}=(D\Pi_{\rm ph})^t.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23N}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_50}
\end{equation}
With \(Z=U+\Pi_{\rm ph}W\), the invertible gauge
\begin{equation}
 G_D=\exp\{-2\pi i(U^tDW+W^tD\Pi_{\rm ph}W)\}

\hypertarget{tag-Q23O}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_51}
\end{equation}
and its reciprocal give short factors one and long factors
\(h_1^{-a}z^{-2a}\), \(R^b v^{2b}\). Multiplication by \(z^a\),
inverted by \(z^{-a}\), gives the exact period and inversion maps
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 h(z,v,\zeta)&=(h_1z,h_G^a v,z^{-2a}\zeta),\\
 F(z,v,\zeta)&=(h_G^{-b}z,Rv,h_G^{-ab}R^b v^{2b}\zeta),\\
 J(z,v,\zeta)&=(z^{-1},v^{-1},z^{-2a}\zeta).
 \end{split}

\hypertarget{tag-Q23P}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_52}
\end{equation}
The line factors of the inverses of \(h,F\), in target coordinates,
are \(h_1^{-2a}z^{2a}\) and \(h_G^{ab}R^b v^{-2b}\);
\(J^{-1}=J\). Substitution gives \(hF=Fh\), \(JhJ=h^{-1}\),
\(JFJ=F^{-1}\). The original functions \(h_1,h_G,R\), with their
units, are retained. On overlaps compose one Gaussian, the actual
integral period change, and the reciprocal Gaussian of the other
chart. Triple composites cancel, so this is one line on the regular
part of \(Y\). Its degree on \(s\) is two and on every constant fixed
loop sweep is zero.

At a rank-one cap use \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_36}. With
\(\Pi_N=C_S^{-1}\Pi_{\rm ph}C_L\), the Gaussian is
\begin{equation}
 \exp\{-2\pi i(U^tK_NW+W^tK_N^t\Pi_NW)\}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23Q}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_53}
\end{equation}
The symmetry required is that of \(K_N^t\Pi_N\); the full off-diagonal
entry \(M\) remains. Its long factors have exponent
\(-4\pi i h^tK_N^tZ-2\pi i h^tK_N^t\Pi_Nh\).
The integer support \(Nj^2\) has difference \(2Nhj+Nh^2\), and
its affine monomials with reciprocal inverses extend the line through
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_39}. The remaining factors are the actual unit functions.
Replacing \(\Pi_N\) by \(\Pi_N+E_{11}\) and replacing \(U\) by
\(U+E_{11}W\) add the same \(NW_1^2\), proving around-base compatibility.

For the nonprimitive physical first period put
\(\alpha_0=d_0\), \(v_0=(n_0,-A_0)\), \(\beta_0=B_0\).
The two physical valuation columns are \(\alpha_0v_0,\beta_0v_0\)
and
\begin{equation}
 a\beta_0(v_0)_1+b\alpha_0(v_0)_2=0,\qquad
 \psi_1(j)=-\frac{a(v_0)_1}{\alpha_0}j(j-\alpha_0)
                         =-Nj^2+N\alpha_0j.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23R}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_54}
\end{equation}
Its two differences are exactly the physical exponents of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_52}.
The linear term is the \(z^a\) gauge and the quadratic sign changes
between frame and vector coordinates. This is the full support
comparison also when \(d_0=5\); it does not replace that first
column by an unrecorded primitive one.
At \(E_p\) the doubled line has degree \(-2b/p\), and its character
is \(-\sigma N(2j+1)=0\pmod p\) for odd \(p\).

At the cusp the symmetric pairing is
\begin{equation}
 K=C^tD=
 \begin{pmatrix}a\nu&ab\kappa\\ab\kappa&b(ab\kappa^2-1)/\nu\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad Kd_\delta=(0,-b)^t.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23S}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_55}
\end{equation}
The Gaussian \(\exp\{-2\pi i(U^tKW+W^tK\Pi W)\}\) gives long
factors \(q^{-h^tKh}X^{-2Kh}\) times their unchanged holomorphic
units. The support \(\psi(v)=v^tKv\) on the prescribed unit triangles
has the required difference \(2h^tKv+h^tKh\).
For a triangle slope \(m\), its regular frame is
\(e_m=\chi^{-m}e\) and vector coordinate is
\(\zeta_m=\chi^m\zeta\); the opposite monomials give the inverse.

The physical support is
\begin{equation}
 \psi_{\rm ph}(x,y)=-a\rho x^2+2ab\kappa xy+b\nu y^2+ax,\qquad
 \psi_{\rm ph}(Cv)=-v^tKv+a\,\operatorname{row}_1(C)v.
 \hypertarget{tag-020K}{}\label{maps:eq:D.MT2}
\end{equation}
Direct expansion proves the identity. The inverse period map
is \(C^{-1}\), the reciprocal frame changes the quadratic sign, and
the displayed integral affine term is the \(z^a\) monomial gauge,
with its reciprocal as inverse. These maps identify the four physical
wall vertices with the actual wall of the prescribed fan. In
particular no physical unit wall is substituted for that transported
wall. On the edge \([0,e_2]\), the slopes are
\begin{equation}
 m_+=(K_{11},K_{22}),\qquad
 m_-=(2K_{12}-K_{11},K_{22}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23T}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_57}
\end{equation}
Their character and degree are
\begin{equation}
 c_{\mathcal D,C}=b\{a\kappa(b\kappa-\nu)-1\},\qquad
 \deg\mathcal D|C_f=2(K_{11}-K_{12})=2a(\nu-b\kappa).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23U}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_58}
\end{equation}
The character is zero modulo every odd \(p\mid c\).
All base units have local logarithms, are scalar invariant, and have
zero degree on the central curve; their actual factors remain in the
maps above.

For the even sectors \(c=6,30\), the table gives \(a\) odd and \(b\)
even. There is an undoubled line \(\mathcal L\) with factors
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal Q(\lambda)=\lambda_1\lambda_2+\lambda_1+\lambda_2\pmod2,
 \quad
 J^\mathcal L_\lambda(x,\zeta_R)=
 (x+\lambda,(-1)^{\mathcal Q(\lambda)}e^{\pi i\omega(\lambda,x)}\zeta_R).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23V}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_59}
\end{equation}
Its polar form is \(\omega\bmod2\). The form \(\mathcal Q\) is one
on every nonzero elliptic vector modulo two; each \(A_i\) is
invertible there, and the elliptic gamma column \(bg_i\) is even.
Thus every actual \(T_i\) preserves \(\mathcal Q\). This proves the
cocycle law and the same scalar-one monodromy lift. Its connection
has Chern class \(\omega\), degree one on \(s\), zero on sweeps.
The scalar lift by \(\delta/N\), \(N\mid b\), has factor
\(e^{\pi i\omega(\delta/N,x)}\), commutator
\(e^{-2\pi i b\lambda_\gamma/N}=1\), and \(N\)-th power \(J^\mathcal L_\delta\).

The physical Gaussian is
\begin{equation}
 \exp\{\pi i[-U^tDW-W^tD\Pi_{\rm ph}W+Z_1]\},

\hypertarget{tag-Q23W}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_60}
\end{equation}
followed by \(z^{(a-1)/2}\). Its reciprocal is the inverse. The actual
maps corresponding to \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_52} have factors
\begin{equation}
 h:\ (-1)^a z^{-a},\qquad
 F:\ h_G^{-ab/2}R^{b/2}v^b,\qquad
 J:\ z^{-a}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23X}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_61}
\end{equation}
Their inverse factors are
\((-1)^a h_1^{-a}z^a\), \(h_G^{ab/2}R^{b/2}v^{-b}\), and the
same \(J\). All unit exponents are integers.

In the cusp coordinates the short refinement is
\(\epsilon=(1,0)^t\), and the support is
\begin{equation}
 \psi_{\mathcal L}(v)=\frac{v^tKv-v_1}{2},\qquad
 \frac12\psi_{\rm ph}(Cv)=-\psi_{\mathcal L}(v)+L_0v,\quad
 L_0=\frac{a\,\operatorname{row}_1C-(1,0)}2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23Y}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_62}
\end{equation}
Both the support and \(L_0\) are integral: \(K_{11}\) is odd,
\(K_{22}\) is even, and the same parity gives \(L_0\).
After the reciprocal frame identification, the dense monomial
\(X^{L_0}\), with inverse \(X^{-L_0}\), identifies the regular
Cartier coordinates. The Gaussian scalar phase adds
\(\epsilon^td_\delta/2\). Hence
\begin{equation}
 \deg\mathcal L|C_f=a(\nu-b\kappa),\quad
 c_{\mathcal L,C}=\frac b2\{a\kappa(b\kappa-\nu)-1\}
                    =1+\kappa\pmod2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23Z}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_63}
\end{equation}
The degree is odd. Here \(b/2\) and \(\nu\) are odd, as seen in every
even-sector row.

At a rank-one site the short refinement is
\(\epsilon_{N,j}=\mathcal Q(SC_Se_j)\); use the Gaussian with
\(\epsilon_N^tZ\) and support
\((Nj^2-\mathcal Q(l)j)/2\).
The physical first content \(d_0\) is odd in these sectors
(one or five), so \(-N j(j-d_0)/2\) is integral.
The difference of the two supports is the integral linear form
\((a\,\operatorname{row}_1C_S-\epsilon_N^t)/2\).
Its monomial and reciprocal give the actual same-line comparison.
Squaring \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_59} gives exactly \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_48}. The supports of the tensor squares differ by
the linear refinement term; the opposite monomial identifies their
regular coordinates. This gives
\(\mathcal L^{\otimes2}\simeq\mathcal D\), including finite actions.

At \(I_2\) use the explicit primitive basis
\begin{equation}
 l=2u-a\kappa_i\delta,\quad
 p_0=u+(1-a\kappa_i)\delta/2,\quad
 h=v,\quad w_0=\gamma-(b\kappa_i/2)v.

\hypertarget{tag-Q240}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_64}
\end{equation}
Its short matrix is
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}2&1\\-a\kappa_i&(1-a\kappa_i)/2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\),
inverse
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}(1-a\kappa_i)/2&-1\\a\kappa_i&2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\);
the long matrix and inverse are
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-b\kappa_i/2\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\)
and
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&b\kappa_i/2\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\).
Compared with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_36}, \(p_{\rm gen}=p_0+(\sigma-1)l/2\) and
\(h_{\rm gen}=h+yw_0\); subtracting these multiples is the inverse.
The pairing in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_64} is
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}2a&0\\a&-b/2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\).
Thus
\begin{equation}
                  \deg\mathcal L|E_2=-b/2,\qquad c_{\mathcal L,E_2}=0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q241}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_65}
\end{equation}
Indeed \(\delta=-l+2p_0\), \(\mathcal Q(p_0)=1\), and the nodal
slope is \(a\); its half-period character is \(1-a=0\pmod2\).
This completes the actual equivariant-line construction on \(Y\).

\paragraph{8. The common global Borel comparison.}

The cusp kills both columns \(SC\), equivalently \(u,\delta\) through
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_42}. Each rank-one cap kills its primitive \(l\).
Let \(K_1\) collect these angular columns in the first three coordinates.
Integer reduction of these angular columns gives the section
\begin{equation}
                              K_1R_1=I_3.

\hypertarget{tag-Q242}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_66}
\end{equation}
Thus \(e_1,e_2,\delta\) vanish in the actual fundamental group:
the fibre starts abelian, and these are cap-disc relations.
The remaining period relations make \(\gamma\) commute with every
unfilled base meridian. With zero or one additive meridian,
\begin{equation}
                         \pi_1(Y)=\mathbb Z\gamma\times F_{r_Y},
                         \qquad r_Y=0\text{ or }1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q243}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_67}
\end{equation}
The inverse presentation kills the three angular generators, retains
\(\gamma\) and the additive meridian, and replaces the ordinary
meridian by their inverse product. Equation \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_66} supplies exact
words for the killed generators. In particular \(H_1(Y)\) is free.

Write \(\alpha_p(s)=1\). Every section sweep has zero image in
\(H_2(X;\mathbb F_p)\) for \(p\mid c\): the toric/original sweeps
bound; at a finite site \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_33}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34} gives \(L=0,D=-be\,s=0\) modulo
\(p\). This includes the gamma-two original III lift. By the cycle
coordinates \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_18} and the torsion-free \(H_1(Y)\),
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_p|Y=\tfrac12\rho_p c_1(\mathcal D)\quad(p=3,5),\qquad
 \alpha_2|Y=\rho_2c_1(\mathcal L)\quad(2\mid c).

\hypertarget{tag-Q244}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_68}
\end{equation}
The inverses of the factors two are the usual Bezout inverses modulo
the odd primes.

The connected scalar circle acts trivially on ordinary cohomology.
The possible outgoing differentials from \(\alpha_p\) in its Borel
spectral sequence have targets
\(H^2(BS^1;H^1(X;\mathbb F_p))=0\) and \(H^3(BS^1;\mathbb F_p)=0\).
Thus each \(\alpha_p\) extends to \(U_p\in H^2_{S^1}(X;\mathbb F_p)\).
Choose a toric endpoint as basepoint; the circle fixes it. The based
isotopy and that section give
\begin{equation}
                 \pi_1(Y_{C_p})=(\mathbb Z\times F_{r_Y})\times C_p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q245}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_69}
\end{equation}
A section from any finite fixed point sends the generator of \(C_p\)
to the same finite factor: its component in the torsion-free first
factor must vanish.

The difference between \(U_p|Y_{C_p}\) and the line class in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_68}
has zero ordinary restriction. In the universal-cover low-degree
sequence it lies in group cohomology; its ordinary group component
vanishes by the injective low-degree map for \(Y\). The remaining
terms are a base degree-two class and products of a degree-one group
class with the actual integral degree-one classes of \(Y\).
All these integral classes vanish on each finite isotropy section
by \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_69}. Consequently the base coefficient is one common number on
all fixed curves. Subtract that multiple of the whole-circle base
class \(T\); adding it back is the inverse. The degree-one mixed
terms are retained.

For odd \(p\), after this one global adjustment,
\begin{equation}
 U_p|C_f=\alpha_p|C_f,\qquad
 U_p|E_p=\alpha_p|E_p+\eta_p\xi_p,

\hypertarget{tag-Q246}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_70}
\end{equation}
where \(\eta_p\in H^1(BC_p;\mathbb F_p)\) and
\(\xi_p\in H^1(E_p;\mathbb F_p)\) lifts integrally.
For \(p=2\), writing \(\tau\in H^1(BC_2;\mathbb F_2)\),
\begin{equation}
 U_2|C_f=\alpha_2|C_f+(1+\kappa)\tau^2,\qquad
 U_2|E_2=\alpha_2|E_2+\tau\xi_2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q247}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_71}
\end{equation}
There is no mixed term on the sphere. Equation \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_70} is subsequently
refined on the full-circle Borel space of \(E_3\), so its order-nine
contribution is also controlled.

\paragraph{12. Full coprime homotopy, detector, and bordism maps.}

Put \(K_p=K(\mathbb Z/p,2)\). The cyclic resolution makes
\(K(\mathbb Z/p,1)\) acyclic in positive homology over
\(\mathbb Z[1/p]\). The path-loop Serre sequence, with contractible
total space and simply connected base \(K_p\), then makes \(K_p\)
acyclic in positive homology over that ring. Exactness of localization
implies that each positive integral homology element is killed by a
power of \(p\). For different primes \(p,q\), Bezout between the two
annihilating powers kills the tensor product, and
\(\operatorname{Tor}(\mathbb Z/p^a,B)=B[p^a]=0\) for \(q\)-primary
\(B\). Direct sums and filtered colimits prove the corresponding
Tor vanishing in every degree.

The reduced Kunneth sequence therefore makes \(K_p\wedge K_q\)
acyclic. The CW inclusion
\begin{equation}
                         i:K_p\vee K_q\longrightarrow K_p\times K_q

\hypertarget{tag-Q24P}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_94}
\end{equation}
is an integral homology equivalence of simply connected spaces.
A first nonzero relative homotopy group would, by relative Hurewicz,
be the corresponding zero relative homology group. All relative
homotopy groups thus vanish. One obtains a based inverse \(q\) on cells:
start with the identity on the wedge, extend its retraction over each
attaching cell using the zero relative groups, and adjust the extension
so that \(iq\) is homotopic to the original cell map relative boundary.
The homotopy-extension property assembles the maps on skeleta.
Thus \(qi=\mathrm{id}\) and \(iq\simeq\mathrm{id}\) relative to the
wedge. This is the inverse at its full CW homotopy scope.
For three primes apply the same construction successively; the same
tensor and Tor argument applies to the product of the first two.

On coefficient groups, let
\begin{equation}
 \theta:\mathbb Z/c\to\bigoplus_{p\mid c}\mathbb Z/p,\quad
 x\mapsto(x\bmod p)_p,\qquad
 j_p(e_p)=\frac cp\left(\frac cp\right)^{-1}_{p}s.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24Q}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_95}
\end{equation}
The inverse of \(\theta\) is \(\sum j_p\). The two composites are
identity by the residue calculation. Apply the Eilenberg--Mac Lane
functor to these degree-two coefficient complexes; in a simplicial
abelian-group model its composites are literally identity.
Composing with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_94} and its inverse gives the corresponding full
wedge/product homotopy maps.

The finite wedge axiom for the spin-bordism spectrum gives
\begin{equation}
 \Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K(\mathbb Z/c,2))
             \simeq\bigoplus_{p\mid c}\Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K_p).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24R}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_96}
\end{equation}
Here $\Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(\mathrm{pt})=0$, by
\ref{maps:src:D.1.11} for coefficient group zero, whose injective
pair $(P,h)$ has zero target. The forward map
is the tuple of coefficient projections. Its inverse is the sum
of the factor inclusions followed by \(K(\theta^{-1},2)_*\).
Thus no mixed spin-bordism coordinate is discarded.

Let \(D_2=2,D_3'=9,D_5=5\). The same maps on degree-six homology
and the positive generators give
\begin{equation}
 H_6(K(\mathbb Z/c,2);\mathbb Z)=
 \mathbb Z/(3c)\langle\gamma_3(s)\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24S}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_97}
\end{equation}
Naturality from positive \(\mathbb{CP}^3\) reduction gives
\(\gamma_3(ms)=m^3\gamma_3(s)\). For the subgroup injection \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_95},
the cube of its coefficient is an idempotent modulo \(3c\).
In the three cases the inverse detector maps are exactly
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|c|c}
 c&\text{forward coordinates}&\text{inverse coefficient modulo }3c\\\hline
 6&(\mu_2,\mu_3)\in\mathbb Z/2\oplus\mathbb Z/9&
                    9\mu_2+10\mu_3\pmod{18}\\
 15&(\mu_3,\mu_5)\in\mathbb Z/9\oplus\mathbb Z/5&
                    10\mu_3+36\mu_5\pmod{45}\\
 30&(\mu_2,\mu_3,\mu_5)&
                    45\mu_2+10\mu_3+36\mu_5\pmod{90}.
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q24T}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_98}
\end{equation}
For example, at \(c=6\) the actual coefficient injections are
\(j_2=3\), \(j_3=4\); their cubes are \(9,10\) modulo eighteen.
At \(c=15\), \(j_3=10,j_5=6\), giving cubes \(10,36\) modulo
forty-five. At \(c=30\), \(j_2=15,j_3=10,j_5=6\), giving
\(45,10,36\) modulo ninety. Reductions to the listed detector
orders are the inverse maps: each displayed coefficient has residue
one in its own factor and zero in the other factors. The computed primary evaluations will determine \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_3} through these maps.

For \(P\), the inverse coefficient maps are
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|c}
 c&\theta^{-1}\\\hline
 6&3P_2+4P_3\pmod6\\
 15&10P_3+6P_5\pmod{15}\\
 30&15P_2+10P_3+6P_5\pmod{30}.
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q24U}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_99}
\end{equation}
These are the inverse maps for the refined primary coefficients.
An automorphism \(s\mapsto us\) acts by \(u\) on \(P\) and \(u^3\)
on \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_97}. The latter is independent of the integer lift of \(u\):
\((u+c)^3-u^3\) is divisible by \(3c\). If \(uv=1\pmod c\),
then \((uv)^3=1\pmod{3c}\), since \(3\mid c\).
Thus the inverse automorphism is multiplication by \(v\) on \(P\)
and $v^3$ on $\mu$.


\paragraph{9. The full order-nine detector and localization.}

Use the coefficient map $e\mapsto e_3$ on $\mathbb Z/3$,
with inverse $e_3\mapsto e$. The induced Eilenberg--Mac Lane map
identifies the positive reduction generator in
\eqref{maps:eq:three-primary-homology} with $\gamma_3(e_3)$.
Under this same map the detector is
\eqref{maps:eq:third-power-detector}, with coefficient map
$\gamma_3(e_3)\mapsto1$ and inverse $j\mapsto j\gamma_3(e_3)$.
The coefficient-one cochain construction is
\eqref{maps:eq:third-power-cochain}, and the left-interval suspension
and its slant inverse are those of
\eqref{maps:eq:third-power-suspension}. These are statements about
the same universal target and natural operation. Applying them to the
classifying map for $U_3$ therefore retains the complete $\mathbb Z/9$
coefficient group. The fixed curves and their restrictions for the
present data are computed below.

Restrict the whole-circle \(U_3\) of the common global Borel comparison to \(C_9\), obtaining
\(U_9\). No \(C_9\)-equivariant lift of \(\mathcal D\) is needed.
For the specified positive generator, let
\(T_9=c_1(\text{positive character})\) modulo nine.
The cyclic resolution has differentials \(g-1,N_9\); with trivial
\(\mathbb Z/9\) coefficients they are zero. Its period-two shift gives
\begin{equation}
 H^*(BC_9;\mathbb Z/9)
       =(\mathbb Z/9)[T_9]\otimes\Lambda(\eta_9).

\hypertarget{tag-Q248}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_76}
\end{equation}
In particular the constants inject after \(T_9\) is inverted.

Use invariant tubular neighborhoods of \(C_f,E_3\). Their sphere
boundaries and the compact complement are free for \(C_9\), by
the prescribed-cusp construction. Their Borel spaces have the homotopy type of the
finite-dimensional quotients, so their cohomology is killed by
inverting \(T_9\). Thom excision and self-intersection give the actual
inverse restriction map
\begin{equation}
            (z_C,z_E)\longmapsto
             i_{C*}(z_C/e_C)+i_{E*}(z_E/e_E).

\hypertarget{tag-Q249}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_77}
\end{equation}
Pullback verifies the inverse through \(i^*i_*(z)=e z\); the remaining
relative class vanishes after localization. This works for the
invariant curves with their actual \(C_9\) actions, even when they
are rotated or translated.

The equivariant integral of \(Q_3(U_9)\) is a degree-zero constant,
namely \(\langle Q_3(\alpha_3),[X]\rangle\in\mathbb Z/9\).
It therefore suffices to compute the two terms of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_77}.

On \(C_f\), set \(A=a(\nu-k)\), \(k=b\kappa\), and let
\(z_C=c_1^{S^1}(\mathcal O(1))\) with north character zero.
The two affine coordinates in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_45} identify its action with homogeneous
weights \(0,-k\), so
\begin{equation}
                 z_C(z_C-kT)=0,\qquad\int_{C_f}z_C=1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24A}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_78}
\end{equation}
The south character is \(k\). After the one common adjustment in
the common global Borel comparison the north character of \(U_3\) is zero, and its degree is
\(A\), so \(U_3|C_f=\rho_3(Az)\) on the whole-circle Borel space.
Its two normal roots, by \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_46}, are
\((k-\nu)T-z_C\) and \(\nu T-z_C\). Their product is
\begin{equation}
                             e_C=\nu(k-\nu)T^2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24B}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_79}
\end{equation}
The coefficient \(\nu(k-\nu)\) is a unit modulo nine. The integral
lift formula \eqref{maps:eq:third-power-detector} therefore gives
\begin{equation}
 \int_{C_f}\frac{Q_3(U_9|C_f)}{e_C}
       =\frac{A^3k^2}{\nu(k-\nu)}=0\quad\text{in }\mathbb Z/9,

\hypertarget{tag-Q24C}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_80}
\end{equation}
because \(3\mid k\). This includes \(k=0\); it does not treat a
rotating curve as a full-circle fixed curve.

At \(E_3\), the actual primitive periods in the primitive rank-one construction give
\begin{equation}
 E_3=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^2,\qquad
 \theta\cdot(z,w)=(e^{2\pi i3\theta}z,w),

\hypertarget{tag-Q24D}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_81}
\end{equation}
with positive first and second directions \(p_0,w_0\).
There is an explicit Borel identification
\begin{equation}
 ES^1\times_{S^1}S^1_{(3)}\longrightarrow ES^1/C_3,\quad
 [e,z]\longmapsto[e r],\quad r^3=z.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24E}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_82}
\end{equation}
Changing the root changes \(er\) by \(C_3\). The associated-bundle
relation \([et,z]=[e,t^3z]\) proves it is well-defined.
The inverse is \([e]\mapsto[e,1]\); both composites agree. Thus
\((E_3)_{S^1}\simeq BC_3\times S^1\). A positive \(p_0\) turn
lifts to a root path ending at the specified generator of \(C_3\),
while \(w_0\) is retained. If \(\eta\) is its degree-one generator
and \(\xi\) the positive integral class of the second circle, then
\begin{equation}
 H^2((E_3)_{S^1};\mathbb F_3)
          =\mathbb F_3t_3\oplus\mathbb F_3\eta\rho_3\xi.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24F}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_83}
\end{equation}
Here $t_3=\rho_3(T)$ is the restricted positive circle character.
The common zero isotropy character removes the first coefficient.
Ordinary pullback of \(\eta\rho_3\xi\) is the positive area class by
the exact generator map just described. The line degree gives
\begin{equation}
                  U_3|E_3=d_E\eta\rho_3\xi,\qquad d_E=-b/3\pmod3.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24G}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_84}
\end{equation}
Let \(q:BC_3\times S^1\to S^1\wedge BC_3\) flip factors and then
collapse the wedge. With the suspension convention of \eqref{maps:eq:third-power-suspension},
\(q^*\sigma\eta=\rho_3\xi\,\eta=-\eta\rho_3\xi\).
Thus \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_84} is \(q^*\sigma(-d_E\eta)\).
Naturality and \eqref{maps:eq:third-power-suspension} show \(Q_3(U_3|E_3)=0\), and hence its pullback
\(Q_3(U_9|E_3)=0\). This kills the entire order-nine class, including
the subgroup invisible to ordinary cubing modulo three.

Combining \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_76}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_84} proves
\begin{equation}
                     \langle Q_3(\alpha_3),[X]\rangle=0
                             \quad\text{in }\mathbb Z/9.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24H}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_85}
\end{equation}
The proof uses the actual global comparison \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_66}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_71} on each new
row and all finite cap maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_25}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34}. Consequently it covers every
allowed multiplicity and affine lift.

\paragraph{10. The other primary cubics.}

For \(C_2\), the normal representation on each fixed curve is complex
rank two with sign characters. Its equivariant Euler class is
\begin{equation}
 e_F=\tau^4+\tau^2\rho_2c_1(\nu_F)+\rho_2c_2(\nu_F)=\tau^4.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24I}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_86}
\end{equation}
Indeed \(c_1(\nu_F)=-c_1(TF)\) is even and a curve has no degree-four
cohomology. Its normal sphere bundles and complement are free; the
same Thom restriction/inverse argument, after inverting \(\tau\),
applies to \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_71}.
On \(E_2\), the integral lift of \(\xi_2\) has square zero, and curve
classes of degree greater than two vanish. Its cubic contribution is
therefore zero. On \(C_f\), with \(h=\alpha_2|C_f\), the only
integrating term of \((h+t\tau^2)^3/\tau^4\) is \(t h\).
The degree in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_63} is odd. Thus
\begin{equation}
                         \langle\alpha_2^3,[X]_2\rangle
                           =t=1+\kappa\pmod2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24J}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_87}
\end{equation}
The removed common character changes this total by its value times
the sum of the two odd ordinary degrees, which is zero modulo two.

For \(C_5\), the normal characters on each fixed curve are opposite
units. Their Euler class is a unit after the base generator is inverted.
Both restrictions in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_70} have cube zero: the ordinary curve term
squares to zero, and the mixed product has square zero because
\(\eta_5^2=0\) and \(\xi_5\) has an integral lift. Hence
\begin{equation}
                           \langle\alpha_5^3,[X]_5\rangle=0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24K}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_88}
\end{equation}
The order-two projection in \eqref{maps:eq:MP_local_61} and the
order-five calculation of \ref{maps:src:D.1.10} give
\(H_6(K(\mathbb Z/2,2);\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z/2\), detected by the
mod-two cube, and \(H_6(K(\mathbb Z/5,2);\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z/5\),
detected by the mod-five cube. Both generators are reduced positive
\(\mathbb{CP}^3\). Thus \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_85},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_87},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_88} are full primary coordinates.


\paragraph{10a. The complete three-primary bordism class.}

Compose the actual classifying map $X\to K(\mathbb Z/c,2)$
with reduction $s\mapsto e_3$. The target group and maps are exactly
\eqref{maps:eq:three-primary-bordism}; its inverse fundamental-class
map is $j\gamma_3(e_3)\mapsto j\zeta$. This applies to the resulting
bordism class of this map because the calculation is a target-group
isomorphism. The full zero coordinate just computed forces $j=0$,
hence also $P=0$. The resulting three-primary bordism class is the
class of the actual $M_{3,0}$ of
Definition~\ref{maps:def:minimal-three-data}.

\paragraph{11. Refined Pontryagin coordinates and exact weight conversion.}

For \(p=3,5\), let the ordinary roots of the two normal eigenlines at
a fixed curve be \(x_1,x_2\), and their characters be \(\lambda,-\lambda\).
Put $t_p=\rho_p(T)$ for the restricted positive circle character.
The tangent root and all products of ordinary degree exceeding two
vanish on the curve. Expanding the two normal roots gives
\begin{equation}
                         p_1(TX)|F=-2e_F.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24L}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_89}
\end{equation}
Both sides equal
\(2\lambda^2t_p^2+2\lambda t_p(x_1-x_2)\).
This does not assume equal ordinary normal degrees at the elliptic
and rational components. Localization gives
\begin{equation}
 \langle\alpha_p\rho_pp_1,[X]_p\rangle
          =-2\{a(\nu-b\kappa)-b/p\}
          =2(b/p-a\nu)\pmod p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24M}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_90}
\end{equation}
The mixed degree-one term integrates to zero. The primary definition
of \(P\) on groups without two-torsion is the quarter of the pushed
forward Poincare dual of \(p_1\); its identification with the intrinsic
class is Zhubr §3.13, printed p.807. Multiplication by four has its
Bezout inverse modulo \(p\). By naturality under the coefficient
projection \(\mathbb Z/c\to\mathbb Z/p\), \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_90} gives \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_2}.
The general odd-group quarter-map is used; the prime-to-six scope of
K8's separate ordinary-cubic formula is not applied to \(p=3\).

Here is the complete conversion to the actual Tate coordinates.
At \(E_p\), \(r=-\kappa_i\) and \(w=(ar)^{-1}=-\sigma\).
The ordinary degrees in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_70} and the degree-zero equivariant
integration give
\begin{equation}
       \frac{a\nu}{\nu^2}-\frac{b/p}{w^2}=0,\qquad
       w^2=\frac{\nu(b/p)}a,\qquad
       P_p=\frac{b/p}{2}\left(1-\frac{\nu^2}{w^2}\right).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24N}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_91}
\end{equation}
All denominators are units on these rows. The physical-weight computation
gives the middle equality from the marked $\kappa_i$ and recovers
\(P_p\) by the last expression and the marking below, independently
of the first expression in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_2}.

The subgroup automorphism \(z\mapsto z^{\nu^{-1}}\), inverted by
\(z\mapsto z^\nu\), changes both normal pairs simultaneously:
the cusp pair becomes \((-1,1)\), and the elliptic pair becomes
\((w/\nu,-w/\nu)\). Its pullback multiplies the Borel character and
degree-one generator by the same exponent. If
\(\alpha_C=(a\nu)^{-1}\alpha_p\), its dual homology generator is
\(g_C=(a\nu)s\), with inverse coordinate evaluation by \(\alpha_C\).
Then
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_C[E_p]=-\frac{w^2}{\nu^2},\qquad
 P_C=\frac{w^2/\nu^2-1}{2},\qquad P_p=(a\nu)P_C.
 \hypertarget{tag-020L}{}\label{maps:eq:D.MT3}
\end{equation}
Multiplication by \((a\nu)^{-1}\) is the inverse rescaling.
The normalizing action and the homology rescaling are distinct
specified maps, both applied with their inverses.

The physical weights in the fourteen patterns are:

\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{l|l|l}
rows & \(p=3\): \(w,\nu\bmod3,w/\nu\) & \(p=5\): \(w,\nu\bmod5,w/\nu\)\\
\hline
1,2,3 & \(2,2,1\) & not present\\
4,5 & \(1,1,1\) & not present\\
6,7 & \(2,2,1\) & \(4,2,2\)\\
8 & \(1,1,1\) & \(4,1,4\)\\
9 & \(2,1,2\) & \(2,1,2\)\\
10 & \(2,2,1\) & \(4,2,2\)\\
11,12 & \(1,1,1\) & \(2,1,2\)\\
13,14 & \(2,1,2\) & \(4,1,4\)\\
\end{tabular}\end{center}

Thus \(P_3=0\) on all fourteen rows. At \(p=5\), the six nonzero
rows have primitive coefficient \(3\) and curve coefficient \(4\);
row 8 and both \(c=30\) rows have zero coefficient. In particular
the criterion is \(w^2=\nu^2\) in one common action marking.

For the two-primary projection use Zhubr's exact quotient
\cite[Sections~3.7 and~3.12, pp.~803,807]{manZhubr75}:
\begin{equation}
 \Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K(\mathbb Z/2,2))
       =\mathbb Z\langle\epsilon,\zeta\rangle/
                        \langle\epsilon,2\zeta\rangle,\quad
 P(\zeta)=1,\quad h(\zeta)=\gamma_3(e_2).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24O}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_93}
\end{equation}
Its forward map is \((A,B)\mapsto B\bmod2\), with inverse the
reduction of \(B\zeta\). The generator is positive spin
\(\mathbb{CP}^3\) reduction and has cube one. Hence \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_87} gives
the actual mod-two projection of \(P(X)\), namely \(t\).
No inverse of four on an even-order group is invoked.
The exact CRT maps now give $P_6=3t$, $P_{15}=3\epsilon$ and
$P_{30}=15t$. Substitution of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_85},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_87},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_88} into \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_98} gives
the full fundamental-class coefficients in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_3}.

\paragraph{13. Marked connected sums, recognition, and the refined count.}

The models in Definitions~\ref{maps:def:minimal-two-data},
\ref{maps:def:minimal-three-data} and
\ref{maps:def:minimal-mixed-data} have their original orientations,
normal framings and primitive generators. The degree-two tube,
degree-three graph and positive conic are the identical embeddings
used in their defining proofs. Their generator identifications are
the identity, with the identity inverse. Those proofs give
$(P,h)(M_t)=(te_2,t\gamma_3(e_2))$, $(P,h)(M_{3,0})=(0,0)$,
and $(P,h)(Z_{5,\epsilon})=(\epsilon e_5,0)$, with simple
connectivity, spin and zero free third homology. We now transport
these complete marked classes through the actual connected sums.

Puncture the model manifolds in oriented discs on which their
classifying maps are constant. Attach the oriented neck, and extend
the maps constantly over the one-handle bordism from the disjoint
union. Its boundary classes add and \(P\) is a bordism homomorphism.
The homology inclusions and the summand collapse maps give inverse
coordinates. The coprime maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_94}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_96} prove that this addition
determines every spin-bordism coordinate.

The precise marking \(\Phi\) from each model's \(H_2\) to the actual
\(\mathbb Z/c\langle s\rangle\), together with its inverse, is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|c|c}
 c&\Phi&\Phi^{-1}(xs)\\\hline
 6&(e_2,e_3)\mapsto(3s,2s)&(x\bmod2,\ 2x\bmod3)\\
 15&(e_3,e_5)\mapsto(5s,3s)&(2x\bmod3,\ 2x\bmod5)\\
 30&(e_2,e_3,e_5)\mapsto(15s,10s,6s)&
                         (x\bmod2,\ x\bmod3,\ x\bmod5).
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q24V}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_100}
\end{equation}
Substitution in each row gives $\Phi^{-1}\Phi=1$ and
$\Phi\Phi^{-1}=1$ on every coefficient.
These markings send the model Pontryagin classes to \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_99}.
Their degree-six maps send the model's sole nonzero fundamental
class \(t\gamma_3(e_2)\) to \(27t=9t\pmod{18}\) at \(c=6\),
and \(3375t=45t\pmod{90}\) at \(c=30\).
At \(c=15\) all fundamental-class coordinates are zero.
Thus both full marked invariants agree with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_1}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_3}.

The recognition scope is that of Theorem~5 and the marked
construction and uniqueness proof in Section~4.1
\cite[pp.~795--796,809--810]{manZhubr75}. Zhubr Theorem 5, printed p.796,
gives the injection
\begin{equation}
 (P,h):\Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K(G,2))
                  \longrightarrow G\oplus H_6(K(G,2);\mathbb Z).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24W}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_101}
\end{equation}
Theorem 3, printed p.795, with the construction and marked uniqueness
proof of §4.1, printed p.809, constructs the representative by killing
the free part of \(H_3\) while preserving its specified \(H_2\simeq G\).
Equality of marked bordism classes then yields a degree-positive
diffeomorphism for the simply connected spin representatives with
zero free \(H_3\). The proof changes the marked bordism into an
\(h\)-cobordism. The rank wording on printed pp.793--794 involves
\(H_2\); the zero-free-\(H_3\) scope here is the stated §4.1
construction and uniqueness proof.

Both the constructed outputs and their models satisfy that scope,
with unique spin structures. Equations \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_98}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_100} and injection
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_101} give equality of their marked bordism classes. The marked
uniqueness gives an oriented diffeomorphism with induced second
homology map exactly \(\Phi\), and its inverse induces the inverse
in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_100}. This proves \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_4}.

All listed types are realized: choose \(e=1\) without a finite cap.
At a finite cap take \(m=d_i,e=1,n=m-1,r=0\) and the affine vector
\(v/m\). It is free, satisfies \(me-n=1\), and is an allowed actual
construction. The cap, line and localization constructions prove the same invariant formulas for
every other allowed finite parameter and affine lift.

Within an even sector, every marking automorphism is a unit, hence
is one modulo two and preserves \(t\). Its inverse is the inverse
unit. Thus the two values of the mod-two cube distinguish the two
types. At \(c=15\), a unit preserves zero versus nonzero \(P\);
the two orders are one and five. Conversely, the explicit models
and maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_100} identify every row with the same listed value.
Different values of \(c\) have nonisomorphic \(H_2\), as their
orders differ, so all six sector types are distinct. This proves
the counts in the theorem.

new_text:
\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Exhaustion of minimal fillings.}

Use the ordered matrices, section cocycles, component coordinates
and heights of Definition~\ref{mapsIII:def:elliptic-marked-data}. For a multiplicative site,
choose the primitive vector \(u\) with first nonzero entry positive and
the Bezout complement \(v\) so that
\begin{equation}
 A-I=\nu u u^tJ,\quad g=\kappa u,\quad
 J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad\det(u,v)=1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22E}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_5}
\end{equation}
The integer \(\nu\) is the content of \(A-I\). If
\(x u_1+y u_2=1\), take \(v=(-y,x)^t\).
For height \(1/d\), a natural selected zero requires \(ab=d/\nu\)
with \(a,b>0\), \(\gcd(a,b)=1\), \(\gcd(b\kappa,\nu)=1\), and the literal
component equality
\begin{equation}
 e_{a\kappa}-e_0-e_{(a-b)\kappa}+e_{-b\kappa}
                =-2e_0+e_1+e_{-1}\quad\text{in }\mathbb Z[\mathbb Z/\nu].

\hypertarget{tag-Q22F}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_6}
\end{equation}
Repeated residues are added. This retains the \(\nu=1,2\) cases.
At each other reducible site one of \(aG,bG\) is narrow. In the marked
component group this means every listed coordinate vanishes modulo its
listed modulus. These are the necessary and sufficient natural-row
conditions of \ref{maps:src:D.2.22}. Its actual map
multiplies the original line vector by the chord and vertical-divisor
quotient divided by the rational base function; its inverse multiplies
by the reciprocal. The small contracting constant and the nonzero
period-unit functions stay in that construction.

The positive-valuation Tate labelling sends the input component label
\(j\) to \(-j\). This bijection is its own inverse. Applying it to every
term of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_6} gives the physical component equation; its right side is
fixed by the reflection. Thus this enumeration retains the physical sign of \(G\)
through the displayed reflection.

An exact enumeration proceeds through every divisor pair and evaluates
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_6} as an integer vector, then checks every unselected component. It
recovers the following 21 natural-row counts, summing to 172:
\[
 \begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrrrrr}
 &\mathrm{OS43}&\mathrm{OS43}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS45}&\mathrm{OS45}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS46}&\mathrm{OS46}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS47}&\mathrm{OS47}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS47}/\mathrm{III}&\mathrm{OS49}&\mathrm{OS49}/\mathrm{II}\\
 \#&6&2&8&4&6&2&14&6&8&10&2\\ \hline
 &\mathrm{OS49}/\mathrm{III}&\mathrm{OS50}&\mathrm{OS50}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS50}/\mathrm{IV}&\mathrm{OS55}&\mathrm{OS55}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS56}&\mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{II}&\mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{III}&\mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{IV}\\
 \#&4&10&2&4&14&6&26&10&14&14
 \end{array}
\]
At a finite additive site of type \(II,III,IV,III^*,IV^*\), respectively,
the finite order and vertical coinvariant index are
\begin{equation}
 (d_i,q_i)=(6,1),(4,2),(3,3),(4,2),(3,3).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22G}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_7}
\end{equation}
The exact finite lattice condition is
\(b(A_i-I)^{-1}g_i\in\mathbb Z^2\). A minimal original additive cap
occurs at \(II,III,IV\) when \(aG\) is narrow and \(bG\) has one component
orbit; the latter is \(\gcd(b,q_i)=1\) in these cyclic cases.
The cap and rank maps of \ref{maps:src:D.2.26} prove these criteria. Finite orders
at different sites are pairwise coprime for a simply connected output,
and
\begin{equation}
                    c=b/\prod_{i\in F}q_i.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22H}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_8}
\end{equation}
Expanding these choices over the sixteen nonstar profiles and
retaining $c\in\{6,15,30\}$ gives exactly the fourteen patterns
in \eqref{maps:eq:D.MT1}.

Only three defining profiles occur. Their ordered fibre lists and
primitive multiplicative markings are as follows:
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|l|l}
 \text{profile}&\text{fibres}&(\nu,\kappa)\text{ at multiplicative sites}\\\hline
 \mathrm{OS56}&I_5,I_3,I_2,I_1,I_1&(5,-4),(3,-2),(2,-1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\
 \mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{II}&I_2,II,I_5,I_3&(2,-1),\ --,\ (5,1),(3,1)\\
 \mathrm{OS56}/\mathrm{III}&I_5,I_3,III,I_1&(5,-4),(3,-1),\ --,\ (1,-1).
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q22I}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_9}
\end{equation}
Each of these profiles has generator height $1/30$ and at most
one additive site. Its matrices and cocycles are the ordered entries
of Definition~\ref{mapsIII:def:elliptic-marked-data}.

\item \emph{Actual period maps and integral exterior coordinates.}

Keep
\begin{equation}
 \Lambda=\mathbb Z\langle e_1,e_2,\delta,\gamma\rangle,\qquad
 \omega=a e_1^*\wedge e_2^*-b\delta^*\wedge\gamma^*,\quad
 \chi=\gamma^*.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22J}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_10}
\end{equation}
Positive \(\gamma\) is the forward contracting arrow covering \(-bG\).
The scalar circle is \(x\mapsto x+\theta\delta\), \(\theta\in\mathbb R/\mathbb Z\).
At a multiplicative site put \(z_i=(\kappa_i/\nu_i)v_i\); at an additive
site put \(z_i=(A_i-I)^{-1}g_i\). Then
\begin{equation}
 \beta_i=-a g_i^tJA_i,\quad
 c_i=\beta_i b z_i+w_i,\quad
 T_i=\begin{pmatrix}A_i&0&bg_i\\\beta_i&1&c_i\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad
 w_i=\begin{cases}1/\nu_i&i\text{ selected},\\0&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}

\hypertarget{tag-Q22K}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_11}
\end{equation}
The inverse has blocks
\begin{equation}
 T_i^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
 A_i^{-1}&0&-bA_i^{-1}g_i\\
 -\beta_iA_i^{-1}&1&b\beta_iA_i^{-1}g_i-c_i\\0&0&1
 \end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22L}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_12}
\end{equation}
Substitution gives integral matrices and both inverse products,
$T_i^*\omega=\omega$, $T_i\delta=\delta$, $\chi T_i=\chi$,
and the ordered product $\prod T_i=I$.

These matrices act on the same actual germs. On a cut base, the period
map is
\begin{equation}
 x\bmod\Lambda\longmapsto\Pi(q)x\bmod\Pi(q)\Lambda.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22M}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_13}
\end{equation}
Its inverse is the elliptic and line logarithm modulo that same lattice.
Here \(\Pi(q)\) contains the original nonzero holomorphic units. An
integral basis \(H\) replaces \(\Pi\) by \(\Pi H\) and coordinates by
\(H^{-1}x\). The inverse uses \(H\) and the inverse logarithm. These maps
retain the base coordinate, zero section, contraction and collars.

Use wedge order \(12,13,14,23,24,34\). Choose \(ax+by=1\) and put
\(s_0=x e_{12}-y e_{34}\). Set
\[
 B_2=[\wedge^2T_1-I\ \cdots\ \wedge^2T_N-I].
\]
Tracked integer row and column reduction gives a section $R_2$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 B_2R_2=I-s_0\omega,\qquad \omega B_2=0,\qquad\omega(s_0)=1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22N}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_14}
\end{equation}
Its columns give the monodromy-cylinder preimages. The inverse quotient maps
are
\begin{equation}
 \wedge^2\Lambda/\operatorname{im}B_2
 \ \underset{n\mapsto[ns_0]}{\overset{[v]\mapsto\omega(v)}{\rightleftarrows}}
 \mathbb Z\langle s\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22O}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_15}
\end{equation}
The difference \(v-\omega(v)s_0\) bounds the actual monodromy cylinders
with coefficients \(R_2v\). There are five unit Smith entries, so no
integral index is omitted.

Include the removed ordinary disc as the last site, with \(T=I\).
For \(D=[T_1-I\ \cdots\ T_N-I]\), the first \(N\) boundary maps are
block inclusions. The last is
\begin{equation}
 j_{N+1}(u)=-(T_2\cdots T_Nu,\ldots,T_Nu,u).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22P}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_16}
\end{equation}
It is obtained by tracing the actual polygon boundary, with its boundary
orientation. Its \(D\)-image is \(I-T_1\cdots T_N=0\).
At every cap take its actual fixed integral basis beginning with \(\delta\).
For $B=[j_iF_i]$, the tracked integer reductions give $V_0,Q_0,R_G$ with
\begin{equation}
 DV_0=0,\quad Q_0V_0=I,\quad BR_G=V_0Q_0,\quad
 \operatorname{im}V_0=\ker D,\quad
 \ker B=\mathbb Z\sum_i\delta_i.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22Q}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_17}
\end{equation}
Tracked elementary integer operations give a saturated kernel \(V_0\)
of $D$; its three nonzero Smith entries are one. The same reduction
of $B$ has unit nonzero Smith entries and nullity one. The displayed kernel vector has coefficient one in every
scalar position and is primitive.

Fixed loops sweep base boundary circles to two-tori. Addition is realized
by the period triangle with vertices \(0,u,u+v\); the opposite triangle
is the inverse chain. Projecting to the graph, using \(R_G\), subtracting
those sweeps and then using \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_14} gives inverse coordinates
\begin{equation}
 H_2(\mathcal E)=\mathbb Zs\oplus
 \left(\bigoplus_i\operatorname{Fix}(T_i)\right)/
                    \mathbb Z\sum_i\delta_i.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22R}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_18}
\end{equation}
A line-frame change on the scalar sweep contributes
\(\delta\wedge\delta=0\). Clutching by \(e\gamma\) changes the polygon
relation by \(-\omega(\delta,e\gamma)=be\).

\item \emph{Cap maps, arbitrary finite parameters, and homology.}

At a rank-one multiplicative cap use the basis \(H=(l,h,p_0,w_0)\)
constructed in the primitive rank-one construction. The actual normalized ruled component with
its two sections identified gives
\begin{equation}
 f_1(x)=(x_2,x_3,x_4),\qquad
 f_2(y)=(y_{12},y_{23},y_{24},y_{34}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22S}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_19}
\end{equation}
Inserting zero in the omitted coordinates and applying \(H\) or
\(\wedge^2H\) gives sections. The fibre kernel is generated by
\(e_{13},e_{14}\), with actual monodromy-cylinder preimages \(e_{23},e_{24}\).
Fixed sweeps bound the normal toric chains.

At the cusp use \(H=(SCe_1,Le_1,SCe_2,Le_2)\) of the prescribed-cusp construction. Then
\begin{equation}
 f_1(x)=(x_2,x_4),\qquad
 f_2(y)=(y_{12},y_{14}-y_{23},y_{24},y_{34}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22T}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_20}
\end{equation}
A section of the second map inserts \((u,0,v,0,w,z)\) and applies
\(\wedge^2H\). The primitive kernel is \(e_{13},e_{14}+e_{23}\);
monodromy-cylinder preimages are \(e_{23}\) and \(e_{24}-e_{23}\).
The normalization/conductor sequence gives these cycles and
the normal chains bounding both fixed sweeps. Substitution in the
displayed sections gives the identity and their Wang-image vanishing.

For an original additive cap, \(h=a(A-I)^{-1}g\) is integral. Set
\(\lambda=-h^tJ\). The line-frame map and inverse are
\begin{equation}
 S_P(x,d,g_0)=(x,d+\lambda x,g_0),\qquad
 S_P^{-1}(x,d',g_0)=(x,d'-\lambda x,g_0).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22U}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_21}
\end{equation}
The identity \(\lambda(A-I)+\beta=0\) removes the line shear on
the actual neighbourhood. Zero line multidegree gives a topological
line trivialization there. The positive-roof mapping torus for the
contracting arrow has a unit-roof parametrization: choose a smooth
nonnegative bump \(b_y\) of integral one on a roof of length \(r(y)\),
choose \(0<k<(\max r)^{-1}\), and use the increasing map
\begin{equation}
 f_y(t)=\int_0^t\{k+(1-kr(y))b_y(v)\}\,dv

\hypertarget{tag-Q22V}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_22}
\end{equation}
and its inverse. The ends have matching derivatives. Translation by
the identity-component Neron logarithm times the roof coordinate,
with inverse negative translation and a cutoff at the ends, removes
that part of the return. The finite component translation has one
orbit. Reciprocal line gauges retain its actual node constants.
These inverse maps identify the original cap and its indicated mapping torus.

Put \(z_Q=b(A-I)^{-1}g\) and \(q=1\) for \(II\), \(q=2\) for \(III\).
The original core has \(H_2\) basis \([C],[\delta\gamma]\), with
elliptic fibre \(q[C]\). In coordinates after \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_21}, its map is
\begin{equation}
 f_2(y)=\bigl(q(y_{12}+z_{Q,2}y_{14}-z_{Q,1}y_{24}),y_{34}\bigr).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22W}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_23}
\end{equation}
The first coordinate is the pullback under \(x\mapsto x+z_Qx_\gamma\),
whose inverse subtracts that term. An integral Bezout solution of
\(qA+qz_{Q,2}B-qz_{Q,1}C=1\), followed by \(\wedge^2S_P^{-1}\),
gives a section on \([C]\); \(e_{34}\) gives the other section.
These two sections recover the stated generators. The fixed basis is
\begin{equation}
                  \delta,\quad\ell_0=(-qz_{Q,1},-qz_{Q,2},0,q).

\hypertarget{tag-Q22X}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_24}
\end{equation}
For original III in row 9 it is
\(\ell_0=(-15,45,0,2)\). Its two sweeps bound the origin disc and
the two-fold forward orbit tube closed by the same logarithm and line
frame. The gamma index is retained.

At a finite cap, \(\ell=(-bz_1,-bz_2,0,1)\) is integral and
\begin{equation}
 m=d_i k>0,\qquad v=n\ell+r\delta,\qquad
 \gcd(m,n)=1,\quad r\in\mathbb Z.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22Y}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_25}
\end{equation}
For finite II, \(\ell=\gamma,\beta_0=\delta,q=1\). For finite III in
row 8,
\begin{equation}
 \ell=(-15,45,0,1),\quad\beta_0=(0,-1,0,0),\quad q=2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q22Z}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_26}
\end{equation}
Integer reduction of $[T-I\mid\beta_0\mid\ell]$ gives a right
inverse and an integral $y$ with
\begin{equation}
 q\beta_0+(T-I)y=\delta,\quad
 N_{d_i}\beta_0=(d_i/q)\delta,\quad
 N_{d_i}\ell=d_i\ell,\quad-\omega(\beta_0,\ell)=b/q.

\hypertarget{tag-Q230}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_27}
\end{equation}
Its last two inverse rows give the primitive coinvariant coordinates.
This distinguishes the gamma-one finite lift \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_26} from \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_24}.

The actual affine representative is \(R(x)=Tx+v/m\). Another lift
\(Tx+t\) with \(N_mt=v\) is conjugate to this one by \(x\mapsto x+h\),
where \((I-T)h=t-v/m\) on the complement of
\(\pi=N_m/m\); \(I-T\) is invertible on that complement. Translation
by \(-h\) is the inverse and commutes with scalar translation.
For the actual holomorphic units on the whole good root disc, let
\(\mathcal Rf(u)=A(\sigma^{-1}u)f(\sigma^{-1}u)\), where
\(\sigma(u)=\zeta u\) and \(\mathcal R^m=I\). If the two affine terms
differ by \(d\) with \(\mathcal Nd=0\), use
\begin{equation}
 h=-\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}j\mathcal R^jd,\quad
 (I-\mathcal R)h=d.

\hypertarget{tag-Q231}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_28}
\end{equation}
The inverse is \(-h\), so the comparison is over the disc and its collar,
with the units present. A peripheral period change uses the actual norm
\(v\mapsto v+N_m\lambda\), with fixed-sweep shear
\(t(u)\mapsto t(u)+\omega(u,\lambda)s\). The inverse uses \(-\lambda\).
The expression \(m\lambda\) is used only for a fixed \(\lambda\).

The boundary map and its inverse in the chosen affine representative are
\begin{equation}
 [y,\tau]\longmapsto[y+(\tau/m)v,e^{2\pi i\tau/m}],\qquad
 [x,z]\longmapsto[x-(\tau/m)v,\tau].

\hypertarget{tag-Q232}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_29}
\end{equation}
A different root argument changes both sides by the mapping-torus
relation. The positive normal meridian is \(m\mu-v\).
Actual sectors of its meridional disc and the period parallelograms give,
in \((s,D=t(\delta),L=t(\ell))\), the full integral Thom columns
\begin{equation}
 C_1=(bn/q,m/q,0),\quad C_2=(-br,0,m),\quad C_3=(0,r,n).

\hypertarget{tag-Q233}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_30}
\end{equation}
The opposite sectors give the inverse boundary chains.

To check their integral scope, the finite central surface has
\begin{equation}
 H_1(F)=\langle\beta_0,\ell,c_0\mid
                  mc_0=n\ell+rq\beta_0\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q234}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_31}
\end{equation}
For \(A_0m+B_0n=1\), the basis
\(\beta_0,z=A_0\ell+B_0c_0\) has inverse
\(\ell=mz-B_0rq\beta_0\), \(c_0=nz+A_0rq\beta_0\).
Thus \(H_1(F)\) is free. The finite torus cover and transfer give rational
Betti numbers \(1,2,2,2,1\); integral duality then makes \(H_2,H_3\)
free as well. The flat normal line has zero Chern class in free
\(H^2(F;\mathbb Z)\). Transfer of \(\lambda\) cuts into sectors with
fibre area \(-\lambda\wedge v\) and sweep \(N_m\lambda\); the torus for
\(c_0\) has fibre area zero and sweep \(v\). Rejoining those sectors
is the inverse chain map. Thom exactness on the basis above gives
exactly \(C_1,A_0C_2+B_0C_3\), with no suppressed index.

There is at most one finite cap. Its full fundamental-group presentation,
after the angular relations of the common global Borel comparison, is
\begin{equation}
 \langle\gamma,c_0\mid[\gamma,c_0]=1,\ c_0^m=\gamma^n,\ c_0=\gamma^e\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q235}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_32}
\end{equation}
Hence simple connectivity is precisely \(me-n=\varepsilon=\pm1\).
In the rows without a finite cap it is \(e=\pm1\).
Choose \(A_0=\varepsilon e,B_0=-\varepsilon\). With
\(K_0=A_0C_2+B_0C_3\), the exact change of column generators is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 K_0&=(-A_0br,B_0r,1),\\
 C_2-mK_0&=-B_0qrC_1,\qquad
 C_3-nK_0=A_0qrC_1.
 \end{split}

\hypertarget{tag-Q236}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_33}
\end{equation}
The ordinary polygon relation is \(D=-be\,s\). It gives \(L=0\) and
\begin{equation}
                   (b/q)(n-me)s=-\varepsilon c\,s=0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q237}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_34}
\end{equation}
Substituting $A_0=\varepsilon e$, $B_0=-\varepsilon$ and
$me-n=\varepsilon$ proves \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_33}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34} for both signs and all
integers $m,e,r$ in the stated domain. For no finite cap the remaining relation is
\(be\,s=0\), and \(c=b\). All cap maps are surjective and \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_17} is
integral, so Mayer--Vietoris gives \(H_2(X)=\mathbb Z/c\langle s\rangle\).
The two cusp vertices give Euler number two, and the other torus strata
and caps have Euler number zero. Duality and universal coefficients give
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_1}. Since \(H^2(X;\mathbb Z)=0\), the complex first Chern class is
zero, so \(w_2=0\); the spin structure is unique because \(H^1(X;\mathbb F_2)=0\).

\item \emph{Primitive rank-one periods and all scalar isotropy.}

At an unselected multiplicative site put
\begin{equation}
 d_0=\gcd(\nu,a\kappa),\quad n_0=\nu/d_0,\quad
 A_0=a\kappa/d_0,\quad B_0=b\kappa/n_0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q238}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_35}
\end{equation}
Primitivity makes \(B_0\) integral and \(\gcd(d_0,B_0)=1\).
Choose \(n_0\theta_N+A_0\sigma=1\), \(d_0x+B_0y=1\), and set
\begin{equation}
 C_S=\begin{pmatrix}n_0&\sigma\\-A_0&\theta_N\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C_L=\begin{pmatrix}x&-B_0\\y&d_0\end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q239}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_36}
\end{equation}
Their literal inverses are
\begin{equation}
 C_S^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}\theta_N&-\sigma\\A_0&n_0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C_L^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}d_0&B_0\\-y&x\end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23A}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_37}
\end{equation}
With \(S=(u,\delta),L=(v,\gamma)\), write
\((l,p_0)=SC_S\), \((h,w_0)=LC_L\). The ordered period basis
\(H=(l,h,p_0,w_0)\) has determinant one and return \(h\mapsto h+l\).
The other columns are fixed. Its pairing is
\begin{equation}
 K_N=(SC_S)^t\omega(LC_L)
       =\begin{pmatrix}N&0\\M&R\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 N=\frac{a\nu}{d_0^2},\quad R=-\frac{bd_0^2}{\nu},\qquad
 \delta=-\sigma l+n_0p_0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23B}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_38}
\end{equation}
Their determinants are one; the displayed products with their inverse matrices are the identity.

The actual chart identification is \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_13} in this basis, followed by
exponentiation of \(l\). Consecutive charts and their inverses are
\begin{equation}
 X=z/q^j,\quad Y=q^{j+1}/z,\quad XY=q;\qquad z=Xq^j,\ q=XY.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23C}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_39}
\end{equation}
The primitive long period identifies successive charts with the original
unit multipliers transformed by \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_36}. The inverse uses \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_37} and \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_39}.
The core is one ruled normalization with zero and infinity sections
identified along the elliptic curve of periods \(p_0,w_0\).

On that elliptic curve, the scalar circle translates the first factor
by \(n_0\theta p_0\). Thus a subgroup \(C_p\) fixes it exactly when
\(p\mid n_0\). Otherwise it acts there by a nonzero translation.
At a fixed elliptic curve, away from the double locus, its nonzero
normal coordinate has a unit character, so there is no extra fixed
point on the ruled normalization. Substitution of the ordered
component data gives, for each $p\in\{2,3,5\}$ dividing $c$, exactly one such site,
of type \(I_p\), with
\begin{equation}
 d_0=1,\quad n_0=p,\quad N=ap,\quad R=-b/p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23D}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_40}
\end{equation}
Call its elliptic curve \(E_p\). In the actual subgroup generator its
normal characters are \((-\sigma,\sigma)\). For odd \(p\), the
positive-valuation Tate residue is \(r=-\kappa_i\) and
\begin{equation}
             w=(ar)^{-1}=-\sigma\pmod p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23E}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_41}
\end{equation}
This uses \(\sigma a\kappa_i=1\pmod p\).

The cases \(d_0>1\) are not suppressed: for example the unselected
\(I_5\) at \(a=5,b=6\) has \(d_0=5,n_0=1\). Its scalar translation
has no nontrivial finite stabilizer. The displayed basis and its inverse identify that primitive
conductor and its nontrivial scalar translation.

\item \emph{The prescribed cusp and the remaining isotropy.}

At the selected site set
\begin{equation}
 \rho=\frac{1-ab\kappa^2}{\nu},\qquad
 C=\begin{pmatrix}\nu&b\kappa\\-a\kappa&\rho\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 C^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}\rho&-b\kappa\\a\kappa&\nu\end{pmatrix}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23F}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_42}
\end{equation}
Equation \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_6} makes \(C\) integral with determinant one.
The actual return satisfies \((T-I)L=SC\), \((T-I)S=0\).
The prescribed Jordan fan therefore has short basis \(SC\).
The physical scalar direction in that fan is
\begin{equation}
                  d_\delta=C^{-1}(0,1)^t=(-b\kappa,\nu)^t.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23G}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_43}
\end{equation}
Let \(k=b\kappa\). For each prime \(p\mid c\), \(p\mid k\) and
\(\nu\) is a unit modulo \(p\). Thus \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_43} is vertical and nonzero
modulo \(p\). Among the three edge directions of the prescribed unit
triangulation, only the vertical one is parallel to it modulo \(p\).
There is one such edge orbit. Its compact curve \(C_f\) is described
by the adjacent actual charts
\begin{equation}
 (x,y,w)=(q/(X_1X_2),X_2,X_1),\qquad
 (x',y',w')=(q/X_2,X_1X_2,X_1^{-1}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23H}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_44}
\end{equation}
Their inverses are
\((X_1,X_2,q)=(w,y,xyw)\) and
\((1/w',y'w',x'y'w')\). Their overlap and inverse are
\begin{equation}
 (x',y',w')=(wx,wy,w^{-1}),\qquad
 (x,y,w)=(w'x',w'y',(w')^{-1}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23I}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_45}
\end{equation}
Consequently \(C_f\simeq\mathbb P^1\) is embedded and its normal
bundle is \(\mathcal O(-1)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)\).
A translation stabilizing a bounded edge fixes its endpoints and is
zero, so no additional quotient of this curve occurs.

The full-circle endpoint weights are
\begin{equation}
           (k-\nu,\nu,-k),\qquad(-\nu,\nu-k,k).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23J}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_46}
\end{equation}
For \(k=0\) the circle fixes \(C_f\); otherwise it rotates \(C_f\)
with tangent weight \(-k\). Its generic stabilizer then has order
\(|k|\). In every case \(C_p\) fixes precisely this curve within the
cusp, with opposite nonzero normal characters. The two triangle
orbits give the two endpoints already on \(C_f\).

The scalar circle is free on every original additive cap. In an adapted
norm the forward arrow strictly contracts. A unit-modulus scalar
cannot equal a nonzero iterate on a nonzero line vector; the inverse
iterate strictly expands. The zero iterate fixes only the identity
scalar. The same-germ roof and logarithm maps of the cap-map construction retain
this conclusion.

At a finite cap a scalar fixed point would satisfy, on its good cover,
\(x+\theta\delta=R^j x\) modulo the lattice. Applying the integral
covector \(\chi\) gives \(jn/m\in\mathbb Z\); coprimality forces
\(m\mid j\). The remaining scalar translation is primitive and is
fixed only for integral \(\theta\). Away from the central fibre the
base rotation already gives \(m\mid j\). This proves freeness for
every allowed \(m,n,r\), including \(3\mid m\), and every affine
lift through \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_28}. The ordinary cap is free by the same primitive
scalar translation.

Therefore, on each relevant row,
\begin{equation}
                     X^{C_p}=C_f\amalg E_p\qquad(p\mid c).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23K}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_47}
\end{equation}
Each prime subgroup has free complement. Every nontrivial subgroup
of \(C_9\) contains \(C_3\), so \(C_9\) is free off
\(C_f\amalg E_3\). Its normal sphere bundles there are free, since
their \(C_3\) normal characters are nonzero. These statements do not
require a semifree full-circle action.

\item \emph{One actual line, its fan support, and its inverse gauges.}

Let \(Y\) be the partial completion at all multiplicative sites,
leaving the ordinary and additive discs open. It contains all fixed
curves in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_47}.

For every row there is a doubled line \(\mathcal D\) on the regular
period cover with factors
\begin{equation}
 J_\lambda(x,\zeta_R)=(x+\lambda,e^{2\pi i\omega(\lambda,x)}\zeta_R).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23L}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_48}
\end{equation}
The inverse is \(J_{-\lambda}\); the cocycle quotient is
\(e^{2\pi i\omega(\lambda,\mu)}=1\).
The connection \(d-2\pi i\omega(x,dx)\) gives \(c_1=2\omega\).
The actual monodromy lift is \((x,\zeta_R)\mapsto(T_i x,\zeta_R)\), with
inverse \(T_i^{-1}\). For \(p=3,5\) dividing \(c\), the lift of
\(\delta/p\) is
\begin{equation}
 (x,\zeta_R)\longmapsto(x+\delta/p,e^{2\pi i\omega(\delta/p,x)}\zeta_R).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23M}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_49}
\end{equation}
Its commutator with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_48} is
\(e^{-4\pi i b\lambda_\gamma/p}=1\), and its \(p\)-th power is
\(J_\delta\). It commutes with every monodromy lift.

For the nonzero short period $\omega_s$, use
$z_E=z_{\rm add}/\omega_s$, with inverse $z_{\rm add}=\omega_s z_E$.
Put $\tau_E=\omega_l/\omega_s$, $\zeta_G=G_{\rm lift}/\omega_s$,
and $z=e^{2\pi i z_E}$; the inverse logarithm is modulo the short
period. Let $\rho_L$ be the forward line-period logarithm.
The theta ratio
$\vartheta(z_E-a\zeta_G)/\vartheta(z_E)$, with reciprocal inverse,
identifies \(\mathcal O(aG-O)\) with its Poincare factors. Shifting
the theta sum gives short factor one and long factor
\(e^{2\pi i a\zeta_G}\). The forward lift of \(-bG\) has constant
multiplier in this trivialization, because its ratio with the
constant-factor lift is a holomorphic function on the compact elliptic
curve. The constant retains its actual base-dependent unit.

In physical bases \(S=(u,\delta),L=(v,\gamma)\), write
\begin{equation}
 D=\operatorname{diag}(a,-b),\quad
 \Pi_{\rm ph}=\begin{pmatrix}\tau_E&-b\zeta_G\\a\zeta_G&\rho_L\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad D\Pi_{\rm ph}=(D\Pi_{\rm ph})^t.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23N}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_50}
\end{equation}
With \(Z=U+\Pi_{\rm ph}W\), the invertible gauge
\begin{equation}
 G_D=\exp\{-2\pi i(U^tDW+W^tD\Pi_{\rm ph}W)\}

\hypertarget{tag-Q23O}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_51}
\end{equation}
and its reciprocal give short factors one and long factors
\(h_1^{-a}z^{-2a}\), \(R^b v^{2b}\). Multiplication by \(z^a\),
inverted by \(z^{-a}\), gives the exact period and inversion maps
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 h(z,v,\zeta)&=(h_1z,h_G^a v,z^{-2a}\zeta),\\
 F(z,v,\zeta)&=(h_G^{-b}z,Rv,h_G^{-ab}R^b v^{2b}\zeta),\\
 J(z,v,\zeta)&=(z^{-1},v^{-1},z^{-2a}\zeta).
 \end{split}

\hypertarget{tag-Q23P}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_52}
\end{equation}
The line factors of the inverses of \(h,F\), in target coordinates,
are \(h_1^{-2a}z^{2a}\) and \(h_G^{ab}R^b v^{-2b}\);
\(J^{-1}=J\). Substitution gives \(hF=Fh\), \(JhJ=h^{-1}\),
\(JFJ=F^{-1}\). The original functions \(h_1,h_G,R\), with their
units, are retained. On overlaps compose one Gaussian, the actual
integral period change, and the reciprocal Gaussian of the other
chart. Triple composites cancel, so this is one line on the regular
part of \(Y\). Its degree on \(s\) is two and on every constant fixed
loop sweep is zero.

At a rank-one cap use \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_36}. With
\(\Pi_N=C_S^{-1}\Pi_{\rm ph}C_L\), the Gaussian is
\begin{equation}
 \exp\{-2\pi i(U^tK_NW+W^tK_N^t\Pi_NW)\}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23Q}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_53}
\end{equation}
The symmetry required is that of \(K_N^t\Pi_N\); the full off-diagonal
entry \(M\) remains. Its long factors have exponent
\(-4\pi i h^tK_N^tZ-2\pi i h^tK_N^t\Pi_Nh\).
The integer support \(Nj^2\) has difference \(2Nhj+Nh^2\), and
its affine monomials with reciprocal inverses extend the line through
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_39}. The remaining factors are the actual unit functions.
Replacing \(\Pi_N\) by \(\Pi_N+E_{11}\) and replacing \(U\) by
\(U+E_{11}W\) add the same \(NW_1^2\), proving around-base compatibility.

For the nonprimitive physical first period put
\(\alpha_0=d_0\), \(v_0=(n_0,-A_0)\), \(\beta_0=B_0\).
The two physical valuation columns are \(\alpha_0v_0,\beta_0v_0\)
and
\begin{equation}
 a\beta_0(v_0)_1+b\alpha_0(v_0)_2=0,\qquad
 \psi_1(j)=-\frac{a(v_0)_1}{\alpha_0}j(j-\alpha_0)
                         =-Nj^2+N\alpha_0j.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23R}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_54}
\end{equation}
Its two differences are exactly the physical exponents of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_52}.
The linear term is the \(z^a\) gauge and the quadratic sign changes
between frame and vector coordinates. This is the full support
comparison also when \(d_0=5\); it does not replace that first
column by an unrecorded primitive one.
At \(E_p\) the doubled line has degree \(-2b/p\), and its character
is \(-\sigma N(2j+1)=0\pmod p\) for odd \(p\).

At the cusp the symmetric pairing is
\begin{equation}
 K=C^tD=
 \begin{pmatrix}a\nu&ab\kappa\\ab\kappa&b(ab\kappa^2-1)/\nu\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad Kd_\delta=(0,-b)^t.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23S}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_55}
\end{equation}
The Gaussian \(\exp\{-2\pi i(U^tKW+W^tK\Pi W)\}\) gives long
factors \(q^{-h^tKh}X^{-2Kh}\) times their unchanged holomorphic
units. The support \(\psi(v)=v^tKv\) on the prescribed unit triangles
has the required difference \(2h^tKv+h^tKh\).
For a triangle slope \(m\), its regular frame is
\(e_m=\chi^{-m}e\) and vector coordinate is
\(\zeta_m=\chi^m\zeta\); the opposite monomials give the inverse.

The physical support is
\begin{equation}
 \psi_{\rm ph}(x,y)=-a\rho x^2+2ab\kappa xy+b\nu y^2+ax,\qquad
 \psi_{\rm ph}(Cv)=-v^tKv+a\,\operatorname{row}_1(C)v.
 \hypertarget{tag-020K}{}\label{maps:eq:D.MT2}
\end{equation}
Direct expansion proves the identity. The inverse period map
is \(C^{-1}\), the reciprocal frame changes the quadratic sign, and
the displayed integral affine term is the \(z^a\) monomial gauge,
with its reciprocal as inverse. These maps identify the four physical
wall vertices with the actual wall of the prescribed fan. In
particular no physical unit wall is substituted for that transported
wall. On the edge \([0,e_2]\), the slopes are
\begin{equation}
 m_+=(K_{11},K_{22}),\qquad
 m_-=(2K_{12}-K_{11},K_{22}).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23T}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_57}
\end{equation}
Their character and degree are
\begin{equation}
 c_{\mathcal D,C}=b\{a\kappa(b\kappa-\nu)-1\},\qquad
 \deg\mathcal D|C_f=2(K_{11}-K_{12})=2a(\nu-b\kappa).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23U}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_58}
\end{equation}
The character is zero modulo every odd \(p\mid c\).
All base units have local logarithms, are scalar invariant, and have
zero degree on the central curve; their actual factors remain in the
maps above.

For the even sectors \(c=6,30\), the table gives \(a\) odd and \(b\)
even. There is an undoubled line \(\mathcal L\) with factors
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal Q(\lambda)=\lambda_1\lambda_2+\lambda_1+\lambda_2\pmod2,
 \quad
 J^\mathcal L_\lambda(x,\zeta_R)=
 (x+\lambda,(-1)^{\mathcal Q(\lambda)}e^{\pi i\omega(\lambda,x)}\zeta_R).

\hypertarget{tag-Q23V}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_59}
\end{equation}
Its polar form is \(\omega\bmod2\). The form \(\mathcal Q\) is one
on every nonzero elliptic vector modulo two; each \(A_i\) is
invertible there, and the elliptic gamma column \(bg_i\) is even.
Thus every actual \(T_i\) preserves \(\mathcal Q\). This proves the
cocycle law and the same scalar-one monodromy lift. Its connection
has Chern class \(\omega\), degree one on \(s\), zero on sweeps.
The scalar lift by \(\delta/N\), \(N\mid b\), has factor
\(e^{\pi i\omega(\delta/N,x)}\), commutator
\(e^{-2\pi i b\lambda_\gamma/N}=1\), and \(N\)-th power \(J^\mathcal L_\delta\).

The physical Gaussian is
\begin{equation}
 \exp\{\pi i[-U^tDW-W^tD\Pi_{\rm ph}W+Z_1]\},

\hypertarget{tag-Q23W}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_60}
\end{equation}
followed by \(z^{(a-1)/2}\). Its reciprocal is the inverse. The actual
maps corresponding to \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_52} have factors
\begin{equation}
 h:\ (-1)^a z^{-a},\qquad
 F:\ h_G^{-ab/2}R^{b/2}v^b,\qquad
 J:\ z^{-a}.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23X}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_61}
\end{equation}
Their inverse factors are
\((-1)^a h_1^{-a}z^a\), \(h_G^{ab/2}R^{b/2}v^{-b}\), and the
same \(J\). All unit exponents are integers.

In the cusp coordinates the short refinement is
\(\epsilon=(1,0)^t\), and the support is
\begin{equation}
 \psi_{\mathcal L}(v)=\frac{v^tKv-v_1}{2},\qquad
 \frac12\psi_{\rm ph}(Cv)=-\psi_{\mathcal L}(v)+L_0v,\quad
 L_0=\frac{a\,\operatorname{row}_1C-(1,0)}2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23Y}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_62}
\end{equation}
Both the support and \(L_0\) are integral: \(K_{11}\) is odd,
\(K_{22}\) is even, and the same parity gives \(L_0\).
After the reciprocal frame identification, the dense monomial
\(X^{L_0}\), with inverse \(X^{-L_0}\), identifies the regular
Cartier coordinates. The Gaussian scalar phase adds
\(\epsilon^td_\delta/2\). Hence
\begin{equation}
 \deg\mathcal L|C_f=a(\nu-b\kappa),\quad
 c_{\mathcal L,C}=\frac b2\{a\kappa(b\kappa-\nu)-1\}
                    =1+\kappa\pmod2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q23Z}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_63}
\end{equation}
The degree is odd. Here \(b/2\) and \(\nu\) are odd, as seen in every
even-sector row.

At a rank-one site the short refinement is
\(\epsilon_{N,j}=\mathcal Q(SC_Se_j)\); use the Gaussian with
\(\epsilon_N^tZ\) and support
\((Nj^2-\mathcal Q(l)j)/2\).
The physical first content \(d_0\) is odd in these sectors
(one or five), so \(-N j(j-d_0)/2\) is integral.
The difference of the two supports is the integral linear form
\((a\,\operatorname{row}_1C_S-\epsilon_N^t)/2\).
Its monomial and reciprocal give the actual same-line comparison.
Squaring \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_59} gives exactly \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_48}. The supports of the tensor squares differ by
the linear refinement term; the opposite monomial identifies their
regular coordinates. This gives
\(\mathcal L^{\otimes2}\simeq\mathcal D\), including finite actions.

At \(I_2\) use the explicit primitive basis
\begin{equation}
 l=2u-a\kappa_i\delta,\quad
 p_0=u+(1-a\kappa_i)\delta/2,\quad
 h=v,\quad w_0=\gamma-(b\kappa_i/2)v.

\hypertarget{tag-Q240}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_64}
\end{equation}
Its short matrix is
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}2&1\\-a\kappa_i&(1-a\kappa_i)/2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\),
inverse
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}(1-a\kappa_i)/2&-1\\a\kappa_i&2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\);
the long matrix and inverse are
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-b\kappa_i/2\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\)
and
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&b\kappa_i/2\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\).
Compared with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_36}, \(p_{\rm gen}=p_0+(\sigma-1)l/2\) and
\(h_{\rm gen}=h+yw_0\); subtracting these multiples is the inverse.
The pairing in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_64} is
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}2a&0\\a&-b/2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\).
Thus
\begin{equation}
                  \deg\mathcal L|E_2=-b/2,\qquad c_{\mathcal L,E_2}=0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q241}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_65}
\end{equation}
Indeed \(\delta=-l+2p_0\), \(\mathcal Q(p_0)=1\), and the nodal
slope is \(a\); its half-period character is \(1-a=0\pmod2\).
This completes the actual equivariant-line construction on \(Y\).

\item \emph{The common global Borel comparison.}

The cusp kills both columns \(SC\), equivalently \(u,\delta\) through
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_42}. Each rank-one cap kills its primitive \(l\).
Let \(K_1\) collect these angular columns in the first three coordinates.
Integer reduction of these angular columns gives the section
\begin{equation}
                              K_1R_1=I_3.

\hypertarget{tag-Q242}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_66}
\end{equation}
Thus \(e_1,e_2,\delta\) vanish in the actual fundamental group:
the fibre starts abelian, and these are cap-disc relations.
The remaining period relations make \(\gamma\) commute with every
unfilled base meridian. With zero or one additive meridian,
\begin{equation}
                         \pi_1(Y)=\mathbb Z\gamma\times F_{r_Y},
                         \qquad r_Y=0\text{ or }1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q243}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_67}
\end{equation}
The inverse presentation kills the three angular generators, retains
\(\gamma\) and the additive meridian, and replaces the ordinary
meridian by their inverse product. Equation \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_66} supplies exact
words for the killed generators. In particular \(H_1(Y)\) is free.

Write \(\alpha_p(s)=1\). Every section sweep has zero image in
\(H_2(X;\mathbb F_p)\) for \(p\mid c\): the toric/original sweeps
bound; at a finite site \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_33}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34} gives \(L=0,D=-be\,s=0\) modulo
\(p\). This includes the gamma-two original III lift. By the cycle
coordinates \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_18} and the torsion-free \(H_1(Y)\),
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_p|Y=\tfrac12\rho_p c_1(\mathcal D)\quad(p=3,5),\qquad
 \alpha_2|Y=\rho_2c_1(\mathcal L)\quad(2\mid c).

\hypertarget{tag-Q244}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_68}
\end{equation}
The inverses of the factors two are the usual Bezout inverses modulo
the odd primes.

The connected scalar circle acts trivially on ordinary cohomology.
The possible outgoing differentials from \(\alpha_p\) in its Borel
spectral sequence have targets
\(H^2(BS^1;H^1(X;\mathbb F_p))=0\) and \(H^3(BS^1;\mathbb F_p)=0\).
Thus each \(\alpha_p\) extends to \(U_p\in H^2_{S^1}(X;\mathbb F_p)\).
Choose a toric endpoint as basepoint; the circle fixes it. The based
isotopy and that section give
\begin{equation}
                 \pi_1(Y_{C_p})=(\mathbb Z\times F_{r_Y})\times C_p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q245}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_69}
\end{equation}
A section from any finite fixed point sends the generator of \(C_p\)
to the same finite factor: its component in the torsion-free first
factor must vanish.

The difference between \(U_p|Y_{C_p}\) and the line class in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_68}
has zero ordinary restriction. In the universal-cover low-degree
sequence it lies in group cohomology; its ordinary group component
vanishes by the injective low-degree map for \(Y\). The remaining
terms are a base degree-two class and products of a degree-one group
class with the actual integral degree-one classes of \(Y\).
All these integral classes vanish on each finite isotropy section
by \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_69}. Consequently the base coefficient is one common number on
all fixed curves. Subtract that multiple of the whole-circle base
class \(T\); adding it back is the inverse. The degree-one mixed
terms are retained.

For odd \(p\), after this one global adjustment,
\begin{equation}
 U_p|C_f=\alpha_p|C_f,\qquad
 U_p|E_p=\alpha_p|E_p+\eta_p\xi_p,

\hypertarget{tag-Q246}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_70}
\end{equation}
where \(\eta_p\in H^1(BC_p;\mathbb F_p)\) and
\(\xi_p\in H^1(E_p;\mathbb F_p)\) lifts integrally.
For \(p=2\), writing \(\tau\in H^1(BC_2;\mathbb F_2)\),
\begin{equation}
 U_2|C_f=\alpha_2|C_f+(1+\kappa)\tau^2,\qquad
 U_2|E_2=\alpha_2|E_2+\tau\xi_2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q247}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_71}
\end{equation}
There is no mixed term on the sphere. Equation \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_70} is subsequently
refined on the full-circle Borel space of \(E_3\), so its order-nine
contribution is also controlled.

\item \emph{Full coprime homotopy, detector, and bordism maps.}

Put \(K_p=K(\mathbb Z/p,2)\). The cyclic resolution makes
\(K(\mathbb Z/p,1)\) acyclic in positive homology over
\(\mathbb Z[1/p]\). The path-loop Serre sequence, with contractible
total space and simply connected base \(K_p\), then makes \(K_p\)
acyclic in positive homology over that ring. Exactness of localization
implies that each positive integral homology element is killed by a
power of \(p\). For different primes \(p,q\), Bezout between the two
annihilating powers kills the tensor product, and
\(\operatorname{Tor}(\mathbb Z/p^a,B)=B[p^a]=0\) for \(q\)-primary
\(B\). Direct sums and filtered colimits prove the corresponding
Tor vanishing in every degree.

The reduced Kunneth sequence therefore makes \(K_p\wedge K_q\)
acyclic. The CW inclusion
\begin{equation}
                         i:K_p\vee K_q\longrightarrow K_p\times K_q

\hypertarget{tag-Q24P}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_94}
\end{equation}
is an integral homology equivalence of simply connected spaces.
A first nonzero relative homotopy group would, by relative Hurewicz,
be the corresponding zero relative homology group. All relative
homotopy groups thus vanish. One obtains a based inverse \(q\) on cells:
start with the identity on the wedge, extend its retraction over each
attaching cell using the zero relative groups, and adjust the extension
so that \(iq\) is homotopic to the original cell map relative boundary.
The homotopy-extension property assembles the maps on skeleta.
Thus \(qi=\mathrm{id}\) and \(iq\simeq\mathrm{id}\) relative to the
wedge. This is the inverse at its full CW homotopy scope.
For three primes apply the same construction successively; the same
tensor and Tor argument applies to the product of the first two.

On coefficient groups, let
\begin{equation}
 \theta:\mathbb Z/c\to\bigoplus_{p\mid c}\mathbb Z/p,\quad
 x\mapsto(x\bmod p)_p,\qquad
 j_p(e_p)=\frac cp\left(\frac cp\right)^{-1}_{p}s.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24Q}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_95}
\end{equation}
The inverse of \(\theta\) is \(\sum j_p\). The two composites are
identity by the residue calculation. Apply the Eilenberg--Mac Lane
functor to these degree-two coefficient complexes; in a simplicial
abelian-group model its composites are literally identity.
Composing with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_94} and its inverse gives the corresponding full
wedge/product homotopy maps.

The finite wedge axiom for the spin-bordism spectrum gives
\begin{equation}
 \Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K(\mathbb Z/c,2))
             \simeq\bigoplus_{p\mid c}\Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K_p).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24R}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_96}
\end{equation}
Here $\Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(\mathrm{pt})=0$, by
\ref{maps:src:D.1.11} for coefficient group zero, whose injective
pair $(P,h)$ has zero target. The forward map
is the tuple of coefficient projections. Its inverse is the sum
of the factor inclusions followed by \(K(\theta^{-1},2)_*\).
Thus no mixed spin-bordism coordinate is discarded.

Let \(D_2=2,D_3'=9,D_5=5\). The same maps on degree-six homology
and the positive generators give
\begin{equation}
 H_6(K(\mathbb Z/c,2);\mathbb Z)=
 \mathbb Z/(3c)\langle\gamma_3(s)\rangle.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24S}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_97}
\end{equation}
Naturality from positive \(\mathbb{CP}^3\) reduction gives
\(\gamma_3(ms)=m^3\gamma_3(s)\). For the subgroup injection \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_95},
the cube of its coefficient is an idempotent modulo \(3c\).
In the three cases the inverse detector maps are exactly
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|c|c}
 c&\text{forward coordinates}&\text{inverse coefficient modulo }3c\\\hline
 6&(\mu_2,\mu_3)\in\mathbb Z/2\oplus\mathbb Z/9&
                    9\mu_2+10\mu_3\pmod{18}\\
 15&(\mu_3,\mu_5)\in\mathbb Z/9\oplus\mathbb Z/5&
                    10\mu_3+36\mu_5\pmod{45}\\
 30&(\mu_2,\mu_3,\mu_5)&
                    45\mu_2+10\mu_3+36\mu_5\pmod{90}.
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q24T}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_98}
\end{equation}
For example, at \(c=6\) the actual coefficient injections are
\(j_2=3\), \(j_3=4\); their cubes are \(9,10\) modulo eighteen.
At \(c=15\), \(j_3=10,j_5=6\), giving cubes \(10,36\) modulo
forty-five. At \(c=30\), \(j_2=15,j_3=10,j_5=6\), giving
\(45,10,36\) modulo ninety. Reductions to the listed detector
orders are the inverse maps: each displayed coefficient has residue
one in its own factor and zero in the other factors. The computed primary evaluations will determine \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_3} through these maps.

For \(P\), the inverse coefficient maps are
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|c}
 c&\theta^{-1}\\\hline
 6&3P_2+4P_3\pmod6\\
 15&10P_3+6P_5\pmod{15}\\
 30&15P_2+10P_3+6P_5\pmod{30}.
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q24U}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_99}
\end{equation}
These are the inverse maps for the refined primary coefficients.
An automorphism \(s\mapsto us\) acts by \(u\) on \(P\) and \(u^3\)
on \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_97}. The latter is independent of the integer lift of \(u\):
\((u+c)^3-u^3\) is divisible by \(3c\). If \(uv=1\pmod c\),
then \((uv)^3=1\pmod{3c}\), since \(3\mid c\).
Thus the inverse automorphism is multiplication by \(v\) on \(P\)
and $v^3$ on $\mu$.


\item \emph{The full order-nine detector and localization.}

Use the coefficient map $e\mapsto e_3$ on $\mathbb Z/3$,
with inverse $e_3\mapsto e$. The induced Eilenberg--Mac Lane map
identifies the positive reduction generator in
\eqref{maps:eq:three-primary-homology} with $\gamma_3(e_3)$.
Under this same map the detector is
\eqref{maps:eq:third-power-detector}, with coefficient map
$\gamma_3(e_3)\mapsto1$ and inverse $j\mapsto j\gamma_3(e_3)$.
The coefficient-one cochain construction is
\eqref{maps:eq:third-power-cochain}, and the left-interval suspension
and its slant inverse are those of
\eqref{maps:eq:third-power-suspension}. These are statements about
the same universal target and natural operation. Applying them to the
classifying map for $U_3$ therefore retains the complete $\mathbb Z/9$
coefficient group. The fixed curves and their restrictions for the
present data are computed below.

Restrict the whole-circle \(U_3\) of the common global Borel comparison to \(C_9\), obtaining
\(U_9\). No \(C_9\)-equivariant lift of \(\mathcal D\) is needed.
For the specified positive generator, let
\(T_9=c_1(\text{positive character})\) modulo nine.
The cyclic resolution has differentials \(g-1,N_9\); with trivial
\(\mathbb Z/9\) coefficients they are zero. Its period-two shift gives
\begin{equation}
 H^*(BC_9;\mathbb Z/9)
       =(\mathbb Z/9)[T_9]\otimes\Lambda(\eta_9).

\hypertarget{tag-Q248}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_76}
\end{equation}
In particular the constants inject after \(T_9\) is inverted.

Use invariant tubular neighborhoods of \(C_f,E_3\). Their sphere
boundaries and the compact complement are free for \(C_9\), by
the prescribed-cusp construction. Their Borel spaces have the homotopy type of the
finite-dimensional quotients, so their cohomology is killed by
inverting \(T_9\). Thom excision and self-intersection give the actual
inverse restriction map
\begin{equation}
            (z_C,z_E)\longmapsto
             i_{C*}(z_C/e_C)+i_{E*}(z_E/e_E).

\hypertarget{tag-Q249}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_77}
\end{equation}
Pullback verifies the inverse through \(i^*i_*(z)=e z\); the remaining
relative class vanishes after localization. This works for the
invariant curves with their actual \(C_9\) actions, even when they
are rotated or translated.

The equivariant integral of \(Q_3(U_9)\) is a degree-zero constant,
namely \(\langle Q_3(\alpha_3),[X]\rangle\in\mathbb Z/9\).
It therefore suffices to compute the two terms of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_77}.

On \(C_f\), set \(A=a(\nu-k)\), \(k=b\kappa\), and let
\(z_C=c_1^{S^1}(\mathcal O(1))\) with north character zero.
The two affine coordinates in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_45} identify its action with homogeneous
weights \(0,-k\), so
\begin{equation}
                 z_C(z_C-kT)=0,\qquad\int_{C_f}z_C=1.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24A}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_78}
\end{equation}
The south character is \(k\). After the one common adjustment in
the common global Borel comparison the north character of \(U_3\) is zero, and its degree is
\(A\), so \(U_3|C_f=\rho_3(Az)\) on the whole-circle Borel space.
Its two normal roots, by \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_46}, are
\((k-\nu)T-z_C\) and \(\nu T-z_C\). Their product is
\begin{equation}
                             e_C=\nu(k-\nu)T^2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24B}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_79}
\end{equation}
The coefficient \(\nu(k-\nu)\) is a unit modulo nine. The integral
lift formula \eqref{maps:eq:third-power-detector} therefore gives
\begin{equation}
 \int_{C_f}\frac{Q_3(U_9|C_f)}{e_C}
       =\frac{A^3k^2}{\nu(k-\nu)}=0\quad\text{in }\mathbb Z/9,

\hypertarget{tag-Q24C}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_80}
\end{equation}
because \(3\mid k\). This includes \(k=0\); it does not treat a
rotating curve as a full-circle fixed curve.

At \(E_3\), the actual primitive periods in the primitive rank-one construction give
\begin{equation}
 E_3=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^2,\qquad
 \theta\cdot(z,w)=(e^{2\pi i3\theta}z,w),

\hypertarget{tag-Q24D}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_81}
\end{equation}
with positive first and second directions \(p_0,w_0\).
There is an explicit Borel identification
\begin{equation}
 ES^1\times_{S^1}S^1_{(3)}\longrightarrow ES^1/C_3,\quad
 [e,z]\longmapsto[e r],\quad r^3=z.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24E}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_82}
\end{equation}
Changing the root changes \(er\) by \(C_3\). The associated-bundle
relation \([et,z]=[e,t^3z]\) proves it is well-defined.
The inverse is \([e]\mapsto[e,1]\); both composites agree. Thus
\((E_3)_{S^1}\simeq BC_3\times S^1\). A positive \(p_0\) turn
lifts to a root path ending at the specified generator of \(C_3\),
while \(w_0\) is retained. If \(\eta\) is its degree-one generator
and \(\xi\) the positive integral class of the second circle, then
\begin{equation}
 H^2((E_3)_{S^1};\mathbb F_3)
          =\mathbb F_3t_3\oplus\mathbb F_3\eta\rho_3\xi.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24F}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_83}
\end{equation}
Here $t_3=\rho_3(T)$ is the restricted positive circle character.
The common zero isotropy character removes the first coefficient.
Ordinary pullback of \(\eta\rho_3\xi\) is the positive area class by
the exact generator map just described. The line degree gives
\begin{equation}
                  U_3|E_3=d_E\eta\rho_3\xi,\qquad d_E=-b/3\pmod3.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24G}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_84}
\end{equation}
Let \(q:BC_3\times S^1\to S^1\wedge BC_3\) flip factors and then
collapse the wedge. With the suspension convention of \eqref{maps:eq:third-power-suspension},
\(q^*\sigma\eta=\rho_3\xi\,\eta=-\eta\rho_3\xi\).
Thus \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_84} is \(q^*\sigma(-d_E\eta)\).
Naturality and \eqref{maps:eq:third-power-suspension} show \(Q_3(U_3|E_3)=0\), and hence its pullback
\(Q_3(U_9|E_3)=0\). This kills the entire order-nine class, including
the subgroup invisible to ordinary cubing modulo three.

Combining \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_76}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_84} proves
\begin{equation}
                     \langle Q_3(\alpha_3),[X]\rangle=0
                             \quad\text{in }\mathbb Z/9.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24H}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_85}
\end{equation}
The proof uses the actual global comparison \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_66}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_71} on each new
row and all finite cap maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_25}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34}. Consequently it covers every
allowed multiplicity and affine lift.

\item \emph{The other primary cubics.}

For \(C_2\), the normal representation on each fixed curve is complex
rank two with sign characters. Its equivariant Euler class is
\begin{equation}
 e_F=\tau^4+\tau^2\rho_2c_1(\nu_F)+\rho_2c_2(\nu_F)=\tau^4.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24I}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_86}
\end{equation}
Indeed \(c_1(\nu_F)=-c_1(TF)\) is even and a curve has no degree-four
cohomology. Its normal sphere bundles and complement are free; the
same Thom restriction/inverse argument, after inverting \(\tau\),
applies to \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_71}.
On \(E_2\), the integral lift of \(\xi_2\) has square zero, and curve
classes of degree greater than two vanish. Its cubic contribution is
therefore zero. On \(C_f\), with \(h=\alpha_2|C_f\), the only
integrating term of \((h+t\tau^2)^3/\tau^4\) is \(t h\).
The degree in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_63} is odd. Thus
\begin{equation}
                         \langle\alpha_2^3,[X]_2\rangle
                           =t=1+\kappa\pmod2.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24J}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_87}
\end{equation}
The removed common character changes this total by its value times
the sum of the two odd ordinary degrees, which is zero modulo two.

For \(C_5\), the normal characters on each fixed curve are opposite
units. Their Euler class is a unit after the base generator is inverted.
Both restrictions in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_70} have cube zero: the ordinary curve term
squares to zero, and the mixed product has square zero because
\(\eta_5^2=0\) and \(\xi_5\) has an integral lift. Hence
\begin{equation}
                           \langle\alpha_5^3,[X]_5\rangle=0.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24K}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_88}
\end{equation}
The order-two projection in \eqref{maps:eq:MP_local_61} and the
order-five calculation of \ref{maps:src:D.1.10} give
\(H_6(K(\mathbb Z/2,2);\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z/2\), detected by the
mod-two cube, and \(H_6(K(\mathbb Z/5,2);\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z/5\),
detected by the mod-five cube. Both generators are reduced positive
\(\mathbb{CP}^3\). Thus \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_85},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_87},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_88} are full primary coordinates.


\item \emph{The complete three-primary bordism class.}

Compose the actual classifying map $X\to K(\mathbb Z/c,2)$
with reduction $s\mapsto e_3$. The target group and maps are exactly
\eqref{maps:eq:three-primary-bordism}; its inverse fundamental-class
map is $j\gamma_3(e_3)\mapsto j\zeta$. This applies to the resulting
bordism class of this map because the calculation is a target-group
isomorphism. The full zero coordinate just computed forces $j=0$,
hence also $P=0$. The resulting three-primary bordism class is the
class of the actual $M_{3,0}$ of
Definition~\ref{maps:def:minimal-three-data}.

\item \emph{Refined Pontryagin coordinates and exact weight conversion.}

For \(p=3,5\), let the ordinary roots of the two normal eigenlines at
a fixed curve be \(x_1,x_2\), and their characters be \(\lambda,-\lambda\).
Put $t_p=\rho_p(T)$ for the restricted positive circle character.
The tangent root and all products of ordinary degree exceeding two
vanish on the curve. Expanding the two normal roots gives
\begin{equation}
                         p_1(TX)|F=-2e_F.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24L}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_89}
\end{equation}
Both sides equal
\(2\lambda^2t_p^2+2\lambda t_p(x_1-x_2)\).
This does not assume equal ordinary normal degrees at the elliptic
and rational components. Localization gives
\begin{equation}
 \langle\alpha_p\rho_pp_1,[X]_p\rangle
          =-2\{a(\nu-b\kappa)-b/p\}
          =2(b/p-a\nu)\pmod p.

\hypertarget{tag-Q24M}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_90}
\end{equation}
The mixed degree-one term integrates to zero. The primary definition
of \(P\) on groups without two-torsion is the quarter of the pushed
forward Poincare dual of \(p_1\); its identification with the intrinsic
class is Zhubr §3.13, printed p.807. Multiplication by four has its
Bezout inverse modulo \(p\). By naturality under the coefficient
projection \(\mathbb Z/c\to\mathbb Z/p\), \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_90} gives \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_2}.
The general odd-group quarter-map is used; the prime-to-six scope of
K8's separate ordinary-cubic formula is not applied to \(p=3\).

Here is the complete conversion to the actual Tate coordinates.
At \(E_p\), \(r=-\kappa_i\) and \(w=(ar)^{-1}=-\sigma\).
The ordinary degrees in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_70} and the degree-zero equivariant
integration give
\begin{equation}
       \frac{a\nu}{\nu^2}-\frac{b/p}{w^2}=0,\qquad
       w^2=\frac{\nu(b/p)}a,\qquad
       P_p=\frac{b/p}{2}\left(1-\frac{\nu^2}{w^2}\right).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24N}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_91}
\end{equation}
All denominators are units on these rows. The physical-weight computation
gives the middle equality from the marked $\kappa_i$ and recovers
\(P_p\) by the last expression and the marking below, independently
of the first expression in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_2}.

The subgroup automorphism \(z\mapsto z^{\nu^{-1}}\), inverted by
\(z\mapsto z^\nu\), changes both normal pairs simultaneously:
the cusp pair becomes \((-1,1)\), and the elliptic pair becomes
\((w/\nu,-w/\nu)\). Its pullback multiplies the Borel character and
degree-one generator by the same exponent. If
\(\alpha_C=(a\nu)^{-1}\alpha_p\), its dual homology generator is
\(g_C=(a\nu)s\), with inverse coordinate evaluation by \(\alpha_C\).
Then
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_C[E_p]=-\frac{w^2}{\nu^2},\qquad
 P_C=\frac{w^2/\nu^2-1}{2},\qquad P_p=(a\nu)P_C.
 \hypertarget{tag-020L}{}\label{maps:eq:D.MT3}
\end{equation}
Multiplication by \((a\nu)^{-1}\) is the inverse rescaling.
The normalizing action and the homology rescaling are distinct
specified maps, both applied with their inverses.

The physical weights in the fourteen patterns are:

\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{l|l|l}
rows & \(p=3\): \(w,\nu\bmod3,w/\nu\) & \(p=5\): \(w,\nu\bmod5,w/\nu\)\\
\hline
1,2,3 & \(2,2,1\) & not present\\
4,5 & \(1,1,1\) & not present\\
6,7 & \(2,2,1\) & \(4,2,2\)\\
8 & \(1,1,1\) & \(4,1,4\)\\
9 & \(2,1,2\) & \(2,1,2\)\\
10 & \(2,2,1\) & \(4,2,2\)\\
11,12 & \(1,1,1\) & \(2,1,2\)\\
13,14 & \(2,1,2\) & \(4,1,4\)\\
\end{tabular}\end{center}

Thus \(P_3=0\) on all fourteen rows. At \(p=5\), the six nonzero
rows have primitive coefficient \(3\) and curve coefficient \(4\);
row 8 and both \(c=30\) rows have zero coefficient. In particular
the criterion is \(w^2=\nu^2\) in one common action marking.

For the two-primary projection use Zhubr's exact quotient
\cite[Sections~3.7 and~3.12, pp.~803,807]{manZhubr75}:
\begin{equation}
 \Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K(\mathbb Z/2,2))
       =\mathbb Z\langle\epsilon,\zeta\rangle/
                        \langle\epsilon,2\zeta\rangle,\quad
 P(\zeta)=1,\quad h(\zeta)=\gamma_3(e_2).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24O}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_93}
\end{equation}
Its forward map is \((A,B)\mapsto B\bmod2\), with inverse the
reduction of \(B\zeta\). The generator is positive spin
\(\mathbb{CP}^3\) reduction and has cube one. Hence \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_87} gives
the actual mod-two projection of \(P(X)\), namely \(t\).
No inverse of four on an even-order group is invoked.
The exact CRT maps now give $P_6=3t$, $P_{15}=3\epsilon$ and
$P_{30}=15t$. Substitution of \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_85},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_87},\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_88} into \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_98} gives
the full fundamental-class coefficients in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_3}.

\item \emph{Marked connected sums, recognition, and the refined count.}

The models in Definitions~\ref{maps:def:minimal-two-data},
\ref{maps:def:minimal-three-data} and
\ref{maps:def:minimal-mixed-data} have their original orientations,
normal framings and primitive generators. The degree-two tube,
degree-three graph and positive conic are the identical embeddings
used in their defining proofs. Their generator identifications are
the identity, with the identity inverse. Those proofs give
$(P,h)(M_t)=(te_2,t\gamma_3(e_2))$, $(P,h)(M_{3,0})=(0,0)$,
and $(P,h)(Z_{5,\epsilon})=(\epsilon e_5,0)$, with simple
connectivity, spin and zero free third homology. We now transport
these complete marked classes through the actual connected sums.

Puncture the model manifolds in oriented discs on which their
classifying maps are constant. Attach the oriented neck, and extend
the maps constantly over the one-handle bordism from the disjoint
union. Its boundary classes add and \(P\) is a bordism homomorphism.
The homology inclusions and the summand collapse maps give inverse
coordinates. The coprime maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_94}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_96} prove that this addition
determines every spin-bordism coordinate.

The precise marking \(\Phi\) from each model's \(H_2\) to the actual
\(\mathbb Z/c\langle s\rangle\), together with its inverse, is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{c|c|c}
 c&\Phi&\Phi^{-1}(xs)\\\hline
 6&(e_2,e_3)\mapsto(3s,2s)&(x\bmod2,\ 2x\bmod3)\\
 15&(e_3,e_5)\mapsto(5s,3s)&(2x\bmod3,\ 2x\bmod5)\\
 30&(e_2,e_3,e_5)\mapsto(15s,10s,6s)&
                         (x\bmod2,\ x\bmod3,\ x\bmod5).
 \end{array}

\hypertarget{tag-Q24V}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_100}
\end{equation}
Substitution in each row gives $\Phi^{-1}\Phi=1$ and
$\Phi\Phi^{-1}=1$ on every coefficient.
These markings send the model Pontryagin classes to \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_99}.
Their degree-six maps send the model's sole nonzero fundamental
class \(t\gamma_3(e_2)\) to \(27t=9t\pmod{18}\) at \(c=6\),
and \(3375t=45t\pmod{90}\) at \(c=30\).
At \(c=15\) all fundamental-class coordinates are zero.
Thus both full marked invariants agree with \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_1}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_3}.

The recognition scope is that of Theorem~5 and the marked
construction and uniqueness proof in Section~4.1
\cite[pp.~795--796,809--810]{manZhubr75}. Zhubr Theorem 5, printed p.796,
gives the injection
\begin{equation}
 (P,h):\Omega_6^{\rm Spin}(K(G,2))
                  \longrightarrow G\oplus H_6(K(G,2);\mathbb Z).

\hypertarget{tag-Q24W}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_101}
\end{equation}
Theorem 3, printed p.795, with the construction and marked uniqueness
proof of §4.1, printed p.809, constructs the representative by killing
the free part of \(H_3\) while preserving its specified \(H_2\simeq G\).
Equality of marked bordism classes then yields a degree-positive
diffeomorphism for the simply connected spin representatives with
zero free \(H_3\). The proof changes the marked bordism into an
\(h\)-cobordism. The rank wording on printed pp.793--794 involves
\(H_2\); the zero-free-\(H_3\) scope here is the stated §4.1
construction and uniqueness proof.

Both the constructed outputs and their models satisfy that scope,
with unique spin structures. Equations \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_98}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_100} and injection
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_101} give equality of their marked bordism classes. The marked
uniqueness gives an oriented diffeomorphism with induced second
homology map exactly \(\Phi\), and its inverse induces the inverse
in \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_100}. This proves \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_4}.

All listed types are realized: choose \(e=1\) without a finite cap.
At a finite cap take \(m=d_i,e=1,n=m-1,r=0\) and the affine vector
\(v/m\). It is free, satisfies \(me-n=1\), and is an allowed actual
construction. The cap, line and localization constructions prove the same invariant formulas for
every other allowed finite parameter and affine lift.

Within an even sector, every marking automorphism is a unit, hence
is one modulo two and preserves \(t\). Its inverse is the inverse
unit. Thus the two values of the mod-two cube distinguish the two
types. At \(c=15\), a unit preserves zero versus nonzero \(P\);
the two orders are one and five. Conversely, the explicit models
and maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_100} identify every row with the same listed value.
Different values of \(c\) have nonisomorphic \(H_2\), as their
orders differ, so all six sector types are distinct. This proves
the counts in the theorem.
\end{enumerate}

The global comparison \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_66}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_71}
and the finite cap maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_25}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34}
hold on all fourteen patterns for every allowed multiplicity and affine lift.

The global comparison \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_66}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_71}
and the finite cap maps \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_25}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34}
hold on all fourteen patterns for every allowed multiplicity and affine lift.

