On a fixed curve for $C_p$, let the ordinary normal roots be
$x_1,x_2$. They and the tangent root are curve classes. The equivariant
normal roots are $x_1+\lambda t_p,x_2-\lambda t_p$, and

\begin{equation}
 p_1(TX)|F=-2e_F.                                    \hypertarget{tag-Q21T}{}\label{maps:eq:MP_local_63}
\end{equation}

Indeed $x_1^2=x_2^2=x_1x_2=c_1(TF)^2=0$ on a curve; expanding
both sides gives $2\lambda^2t_p^2+2\lambda t_p(x_1-x_2)$.

new_text:
On a fixed curve $F_{\rm fix}$ for $C_p$, let the ordinary normal roots be
$x_1,x_2$. They and the tangent root are curve classes. The equivariant
normal roots are $x_1+\lambda t_p,x_2-\lambda t_p$, and

\begin{equation}
 p_1(TX)|F_{\rm fix}=-2e_{F_{\rm fix}}.                                    \hypertarget{tag-Q21T}{}\label{maps:eq:MP_local_63}
\end{equation}

Indeed $x_1^2=x_2^2=x_1x_2=c_1(TF_{\rm fix})^2=0$ on a curve; expanding
both sides gives $2\lambda^2t_p^2+2\lambda t_p(x_1-x_2)$.

