whose inverse subtracts that term. An integral B\'ezout solution of
\(qA_{\rm Bez}+qz_{Q,2}B_{\rm Bez}-qz_{Q,1}C_{\rm Bez}=1\), followed by \(\wedge^2S_P^{-1}\),
gives a section on \([C]\); \(e_{34}\) gives the other section.
These two sections recover the stated generators. The fixed basis is
\begin{equation}
                  \delta,\quad\ell_0=(-qz_{Q,1},-qz_{Q,2},0,q).
\hypertarget{tag-Q22X}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_24}
\end{equation}
For original III in row 9 it is
\(\ell_0=(-15,45,0,2)\). Its two sweeps bound the origin disc and
the two-fold forward orbit tube closed by the same logarithm and line
frame. The gamma coordinate of this primitive return is two.

At a finite cap, \(\ell=(-bz_1,-bz_2,0,1)\) is integral and
\begin{equation}
 m=d_i k>0,\qquad v=n\ell+r\delta,\qquad
 \gcd(m,n)=1,\quad r\in\mathbb Z.
\hypertarget{tag-Q22Y}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_25}
\end{equation}
For finite II, \(\ell=\gamma,\beta_0=\delta,q=1\). For finite III in
row 8,
\begin{equation}
 \ell=(-15,45,0,1),\quad\beta_0=(0,-1,0,0),\quad q=2.
\hypertarget{tag-Q22Z}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_26}
\end{equation}
Integer reduction of $[T-I\mid\beta_0\mid\ell]$ gives a right
inverse and an integral $y$ with
\begin{equation}
 q\beta_0+(T-I)y=\delta,\quad
 N_{d_i}\beta_0=(d_i/q)\delta,\quad
 N_{d_i}\ell=d_i\ell,\quad-\omega(\beta_0,\ell)=b/q.
\hypertarget{tag-Q230}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_27}
\end{equation}
Its last two inverse rows give the primitive coinvariant coordinates.
This distinguishes the gamma-one finite lift \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_26} from \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_24}.

The affine representative is \(R_{\rm aff}(x)=Tx+v/m\). Another lift
\(Tx+t\) with \(N_mt=v\) is conjugate to this one by \(x\mapsto x+h\),
where \((I-T)h=t-v/m\) on the complement of
\(\pi=N_m/m\); \(I-T\) is invertible on that complement. Translation
by \(-h\) is the inverse and commutes with scalar translation.
For the holomorphic units on the whole good root disc, let
\(\mathcal Rf(u)=A(\sigma^{-1}u)f(\sigma^{-1}u)\), where
\(\sigma(u)=\zeta u\) and \(\mathcal R^m=I\). If the two affine terms
differ by \(d\) with \(\mathcal Nd=0\), use
\begin{equation}
 h=-\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}j\mathcal R^jd,\quad
 (I-\mathcal R)h=d.
\hypertarget{tag-Q231}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_28}
\end{equation}
The inverse is \(-h\), so the comparison is over the disc and its collar,
with the units present. A peripheral period change uses the norm
\(v\mapsto v+N_m\lambda\), with fixed-sweep shear
\(t(u)\mapsto t(u)+\omega(u,\lambda)s\). The inverse uses \(-\lambda\).
On the fixed lattice, $N_m\lambda=m\lambda$.

The boundary map and its inverse in the chosen affine representative are
\begin{equation}
 [y,\tau]\longmapsto[y+(\tau/m)v,e^{2\pi i\tau/m}],\qquad
 [x,z]\longmapsto[x-(\tau/m)v,\tau].
\hypertarget{tag-Q232}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_29}
\end{equation}
A different root argument changes both sides by the mapping-torus
relation. The positive normal meridian is \(m\mu-v\).
Sectors of its meridional disc and the period parallelograms give,
in \((s,D_\delta=t(\delta),L_\ell=t(\ell))\), the full integral Thom columns
\begin{equation}
 C_1=(bn/q,m/q,0),\quad C_2=(-br,0,m),\quad C_3=(0,r,n).
\hypertarget{tag-Q233}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_30}
\end{equation}
The opposite sectors give the inverse boundary chains.

To check their integral scope, the finite central surface \(F_{\rm fin}\) has
\begin{equation}
 H_1(F_{\rm fin})=\langle\beta_0,\ell,c_0\mid
                  mc_0=n\ell+rq\beta_0\rangle.
\hypertarget{tag-Q234}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_31}
\end{equation}
For \(A_{\rm fin}m+B_{\rm fin}n=1\), the basis
\(\beta_0,z=A_{\rm fin}\ell+B_{\rm fin}c_0\) has inverse
\(\ell=mz-B_{\rm fin}rq\beta_0\), \(c_0=nz+A_{\rm fin}rq\beta_0\).
Thus \(H_1(F_{\rm fin})\) is free. The finite torus cover and transfer give rational
Betti numbers \(1,2,2,2,1\); integral duality then makes \(H_2,H_3\)
free as well. The flat normal line has zero Chern class in free
\(H^2(F_{\rm fin};\mathbb Z)\). Transfer of \(\lambda\) cuts into sectors with
fibre area \(-\lambda\wedge v\) and sweep \(N_m\lambda\); the torus for
\(c_0\) has fibre area zero and sweep \(v\). Rejoining those sectors
is the inverse chain map. Thom exactness on the basis above gives
exactly the integral subgroup generated by \(C_1,A_{\rm fin}C_2+B_{\rm fin}C_3\).

There is at most one finite cap. Its full fundamental-group presentation,
after the angular relations of the common global Borel comparison, is
\begin{equation}
 \langle\gamma,c_0\mid[\gamma,c_0]=1,\ c_0^m=\gamma^n,\ c_0=\gamma^e\rangle.
\hypertarget{tag-Q235}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_32}
\end{equation}
Hence simple connectivity is precisely \(me-n=\varepsilon=\pm1\).
In the rows without a finite cap it is \(e=\pm1\).
Choose \(A_{\rm fin}=\varepsilon e,B_{\rm fin}=-\varepsilon\). With
\(K_0=A_{\rm fin}C_2+B_{\rm fin}C_3\), the exact change of column generators is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 K_0&=(-A_{\rm fin}br,B_{\rm fin}r,1),\\
 C_2-mK_0&=-B_{\rm fin}qrC_1,\qquad
 C_3-nK_0=A_{\rm fin}qrC_1.
 \end{split}
\hypertarget{tag-Q236}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_33}
\end{equation}
The ordinary polygon relation is \(D_\delta=-be\,s\). It gives \(L_\ell=0\) and
\begin{equation}
                   (b/q)(n-me)s=-\varepsilon c\,s=0.
\hypertarget{tag-Q237}{}\label{maps:eq:MT_local_34}
\end{equation}
Substituting $A_{\rm fin}=\varepsilon e$, $B_{\rm fin}=-\varepsilon$ and
$me-n=\varepsilon$ proves \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_33}--\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_34} for both signs and all
integers $m,e,r$ in the stated domain. For no finite cap the remaining relation is
\(be\,s=0\), and \(c=b\). All cap maps are surjective and \eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_17} is
integral, so Mayer--Vietoris gives \(H_2(X)=\mathbb Z/c\langle s\rangle\).
The two cusp vertices give Euler number two, and the other torus strata
and caps have Euler number zero. Duality and universal coefficients give
\eqref{maps:eq:MT_local_1}. Since \(H^2(X;\mathbb Z)=0\), the complex first Chern class is
zero, so \(w_2=0\); the spin structure is unique because \(H^1(X;\mathbb F_2)=0\).

