If \(x^py^q-1=(x-1)S_x+(y-1)S_y\), the angular vector \((a,b)\)
closes to the \(I^2\)-class with coefficients
\begin{equation}
             (aS_x,\ bS_x+aS_y,\ bS_y)                   \hypertarget{tag-021A}{}\label{maps:eq:D.OF12}
\end{equation}
on \(((x-1)^2,(x-1)(y-1),(y-1)^2)\).
Its Picard columns are
\begin{equation}
 x^{-1}y^{-2}E_2+y^{-1}E_3,\quad
       -x^{-1}y^{-1}E_2,\quad
       x^{-1}E_1+x^{-2}y^{-1}E_2.                        \hypertarget{tag-021B}{}\label{maps:eq:D.OF13}
\end{equation}
In the present positive component labels \(x\) translates by
\((-1,0)\), and \(y\) by \((0,1)\); the path and angular exponents
are changed simultaneously. Substituting \eqref{maps:eq:D.OF1} into all the
shifted columns gives the integer values
\begin{equation}
                         \begin{pmatrix}9&24\\0&0\end{pmatrix}
                  \equiv E_{\rm sl}\pmod8.                       \hypertarget{tag-021C}{}\label{maps:eq:D.OF14}
\end{equation}
The path dependence in \(S_x,S_y\) is a
Hilbert--Burch relation, which identifies the same integral annulus
class for every path choice.

