For $c\ne0$, the orbit relations are
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-0206}{}\label{maps:eq:D.IC9}
 e_{i+1}-e_i+c_jt=0 .
\end{equation}
Elimination gives $e_i=e_0-i c_jt$ and $l_jc_jt=0$ on each orbit.

new_text:
On $O_j$, write $\varepsilon_{j,i}=\varepsilon_{i_{j,i}}$.
For $c\ne0$, the orbit relations are
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-0206}{}\label{maps:eq:D.IC9}
 \varepsilon_{j,i+1}-\varepsilon_{j,i}+c_jt=0 .
\end{equation}
Elimination gives $\varepsilon_{j,i}=\varepsilon_{j,0}-i c_jt$ and $l_jc_jt=0$ on each orbit.

For $z_{\rm aug}=x-sRx$ and a representative $a_{\rm Wang}$ of the displayed residue,
$k_{\rm Wang}=(c^t\mathcal K z_{\rm aug}+a_{\rm Wang}g)/h$ is integral and
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
 x-(sRx-a_{\rm Wang}A_0\beta)
   =A_0(\mathcal K z_{\rm aug}+a_{\rm Wang}\beta-k_{\rm Wang}\eta),\\
 c^t(\mathcal K z_{\rm aug}+a_{\rm Wang}\beta-k_{\rm Wang}\eta)=0 .
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
These are the two inverse identities.

old_text:
A section exists exactly when $\gcd(g,D)=1$:
the scalar image of $1$ must satisfy $b\equiv1\pmod g$ and
$D\mid b$. For coprime $g,D$, choose $aD+bg=1$. The section is
$[n]_g\mapsto(0,[aDn]_h)$; the full splitting and inverse are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 ((u,[v]_D),[n]_g)&\longmapsto(u,[gv+aDn]_h),\\
 (u,[t]_h)&\longmapsto
 \left((u,[(t-aDn)/g]_D),[n]_g\right),\quad n\equiv t\pmod g .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
All changes of representatives give the indicated moduli.

new_text:
A section exists exactly when $\gcd(g,D)=1$:
the scalar image of $1$ must satisfy $b_{\rm sec}\equiv1\pmod g$ and
$D\mid b_{\rm sec}$. For coprime $g,D$, choose $a_{\rm split}D+b_{\rm split}g=1$. The section is
$[n]_g\mapsto(0,[a_{\rm split}Dn]_h)$; the full splitting and inverse are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 ((u,[v]_D),[n]_g)&\longmapsto(u,[gv+a_{\rm split}Dn]_h),\\
 (u,[t]_h)&\longmapsto
 \left((u,[(t-a_{\rm split}Dn)/g]_D),[n]_g\right),\quad n\equiv t\pmod g .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
All changes of representatives give the indicated moduli.

For a nonzero row $r=(r_1,\ldots,r_k)$, first interchange the first
column and the least-indexed column with nonzero entry. The inverse is
the same transposition. Multiply the first column by the sign of its
entry; that diagonal sign map is also its own inverse. The first entry
is now positive. For $k=1$ these operations already give the required
matrix and its inverse. For $k\geq2$, successively combine the positive
first entry $a_{\rm col}$ and each next entry $b_{\rm col}$. Choose integers
$s_{\rm Bez},t_{\rm Bez}$ with $s_{\rm Bez}a_{\rm col}+t_{\rm Bez}b_{\rm col}=d_{\rm Bez}=\gcd(a_{\rm col},b_{\rm col})>0$, and multiply those two columns
by
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
 E_{\rm Bez}=\begin{pmatrix}s_{\rm Bez}&-b_{\rm col}/d_{\rm Bez}\\t_{\rm Bez}&a_{\rm col}/d_{\rm Bez}\end{pmatrix},\\
 E_{\rm Bez}^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}a_{\rm col}/d_{\rm Bez}&b_{\rm col}/d_{\rm Bez}\\-t_{\rm Bez}&s_{\rm Bez}\end{pmatrix}.
\end{gathered}
 \label{maps:eq:IC_local_31}
\end{equation}
Both matrices are integral and have determinant 1. The product
$V_{\rm alg}$ and the product of these inverse matrices satisfy
\begin{equation}
 rV_{\rm alg}=(\gcd(r),0,\ldots,0),\qquad V_{\rm alg}V_{\rm alg}^{-1}=V_{\rm alg}^{-1}V_{\rm alg}=I .
 \label{maps:eq:IC_local_32}
\end{equation}
The initial transposition and sign map are included in $V_{\rm alg}$ and $V_{\rm alg}^{-1}$.
The last columns of $V_{\rm alg}$, with the last coordinates of $V_{\rm alg}^{-1}$
as inverse, give an integral basis and inverse for $\ker r$.
For the zero row take $V_{\rm alg}=I$ and retain all columns.

old_text:
Apply this first to $d^t$. Denote the resulting kernel basis by
$J:\mathbb Z^k\to\ker d^t$, with inverse $J^{-1}$ on that kernel;
$k=\rho-1$ for $c\ne0$, and $k=\rho$ for $c=0$.
The vector $b=J^{-1}w$ is primitive. Indeed a common divisor of
its coordinates would divide every coordinate of $w=Jb$.
Apply \eqref{maps:eq:IC_local_31}--\eqref{maps:eq:IC_local_32} to $b^t$, obtaining $b^tV=(1,0,\ldots,0)$.
Then
\begin{equation}
 L=J\,V^{-t},\qquad L^{-1}=V^tJ^{-1}
 \label{maps:eq:IC_local_33}
\end{equation}
are inverse integral bases of $\ker d^t$, and the first column
of $L$ is exactly $w$.
Dropping the first coordinate of $L^{-1}$, with inverse given by
the remaining columns of $L$, is the exact quotient
\begin{equation}
 \ker d^t/\mathbb Zw\cong\mathbb Z^{k-1}.
 \label{maps:eq:IC_local_34}
\end{equation}
These maps give the quotient and its inverse over the integers.

new_text:
Apply this first to $d^t$. Denote the resulting kernel basis by
$J:\mathbb Z^{k_{\rm ker}}\to\ker d^t$, with inverse $J^{-1}$ on that kernel;
$k_{\rm ker}=\rho-1$ for $c\ne0$, and $k_{\rm ker}=\rho$ for $c=0$.
The vector $b_{\rm prim}=J^{-1}w$ is primitive. Indeed a common divisor of
its coordinates would divide every coordinate of $w=Jb_{\rm prim}$.
Apply \eqref{maps:eq:IC_local_31}--\eqref{maps:eq:IC_local_32} to $b_{\rm prim}^t$, obtaining $b_{\rm prim}^tV_{\rm prim}=(1,0,\ldots,0)$.
Then
\begin{equation}
 L=J\,V_{\rm prim}^{-t},\qquad L^{-1}=V_{\rm prim}^tJ^{-1}
 \label{maps:eq:IC_local_33}
\end{equation}
are inverse integral bases of $\ker d^t$, and the first column
of $L$ is exactly $w$.
Dropping the first coordinate of $L^{-1}$, with inverse given by
the remaining columns of $L$, is the exact quotient
\begin{equation}
 \ker d^t/\mathbb Zw\cong\mathbb Z^{k_{\rm ker}-1}.
 \label{maps:eq:IC_local_34}
\end{equation}
These maps give the quotient and its inverse over the integers.

old_text:
From the trivial line with its constant frame, the canonical tensor map
on the punctured neighbourhood and its inverse are
\begin{equation}
 I(p,w)=(p,u(p)w),\qquad I^{-1}(p,W)=(p,W/u(p)).
\end{equation}
Writing $\mu_0$ for the meridian in the extending frame, these maps give
\begin{equation}
 I_*\mu=\mu_0+\delta,\quad I_*\delta=\delta,\quad I_*\gamma=\gamma,
 \qquad I_*^{-1}\mu_0=\mu-\delta .
\end{equation}
Here $q_Q=1,z=0,\ell=\gamma$. The torus
$\gamma\wedge\mu_0$ bounds the section disc times the long circle, so
the marked second normal has image
\begin{equation}
 [\gamma\wedge\mu]=[\gamma\wedge(\mu_0+\delta)]=-t .
\end{equation}
The inverse meridian change contributes $+t$.

new_text:
From the trivial line with its constant frame, the canonical tensor map
on the punctured neighbourhood and its inverse are
\begin{equation}
 I_{\rm tens}(p,w)=(p,u(p)w),\qquad I_{\rm tens}^{-1}(p,W)=(p,W/u(p)).
\end{equation}
Writing $\mu_{\rm mer}$ for the source meridian and $\mu_{0,{\rm mer}}$
for the meridian in the extending frame, these maps give
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
 (I_{\rm tens})_*\mu_{\rm mer}=\mu_{0,{\rm mer}}+\delta,\quad
 (I_{\rm tens})_*\delta=\delta,\quad (I_{\rm tens})_*\gamma=\gamma,\\
 (I_{\rm tens})_*^{-1}\mu_{0,{\rm mer}}=\mu_{\rm mer}-\delta .
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
Here $q_Q=1,z=0,\ell=\gamma$. The torus
$\gamma\wedge\mu_{0,{\rm mer}}$ bounds the section disc times the long circle, so
the marked second normal has image
\begin{equation}
 [\gamma\wedge\mu_{\rm mer}]=[\gamma\wedge(\mu_{0,{\rm mer}}+\delta)]=-t .
\end{equation}
The inverse meridian change contributes $+t$.

